Rem ==================================================================
Rem reckoner.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: the runtime engine
Rem
Rem An interpreter for whitespace-tokenized RPN lines, evaluated against
Rem a dictionary of registered words over cuttle.shoddy's runtime stack.
Rem It is general infrastructure and not a calculator: a formula read
Rem from a spec file, an expression typed into a form field and an
Rem embedded scripting layer are equally its callers, which is why it is
Rem not named for the showpiece shell.
Rem
Rem A READY RECKONER was the printed table of pre-computed answers every
Rem cloth merchant and mill clerk worked from to price a piece by length
Rem and weight — the trade's calculating instrument before there were
Rem calculators. "Reckon" is the plain regional word for compute besides.
Rem
Rem NOTHING KEYABLE ABORTS THE MILL. That is the load-bearing contract
Rem and the reason every word here answers with a value: underflow, a
Rem missing arity, an unknown word, a kind that has no arithmetic, a
Rem divisor of zero, a domain a builtin would Error on. Losing the
Rem stack, the dictionary, the registers and the tape is worse than any
Rem wrong answer, so every guard is written before the builtin it
Rem protects rather than after it.
Rem
Rem THE ENGINE PERFORMS NO EFFECT. It never reads, writes or prints. It
Rem applies words whose bodies may, and that is a property of the WORD,
Rem declared where the word is registered — RckPure or RckTouch — and
Rem reported by HELP. Evaluation returns lines for a shell to print; the
Rem engine never touches the terminal.
Rem
Rem IT KNOWS NO DOMAIN. There is no Include of stats, csv, file or any
Rem other machine, and no word here names one. Every domain arrives
Rem through RckReg as a seed file, which is what makes this machine
Rem independent of any domain's timeline. What it does include is
Rem cuttle, for the value set every seed must speak, and seq/str/dict
Rem for the plumbing.
Rem
Rem THREE THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE READING THE EVALUATOR:
Rem
Rem   A SUB-EVALUATION GETS A FRESH STACK AND THREADS THE STATE. A
Rem   { ... } literal, a combinator's program and a user word's body all
Rem   run on a stack of their own, while registers, dictionary and modes
Rem   thread through and their changes persist. Token lists have no
Rem   closures, so STO before and RCL inside is the only way a program
Rem   reaches a value that is not its element — that is the closure
Rem   substitute, and it only works if the threading is guaranteed.
Rem
Rem   A USER WORD TAKES EXACTLY ONE CELL. Its fresh stack is seeded with
Rem   the one cell it was applied to, and whatever it leaves is pushed
Rem   back. RckDef carries no arity because there is nothing to carry:
Rem   one element in, by the same rule that gives a MAP program its
Rem   element, and a second argument is banked in a register first.
Rem
Rem   THE LINE IS THE TRANSACTION. A whole line evaluates or the state
Rem   stands; the error case hands back the state exactly as it was, so
Rem   a shell's loop never loses the user's stack. An effectful word that
Rem   already wrote a file has still written it — UNDO restores a stack,
Rem   not the world, and no REPL's undo ever promised otherwise.
Rem
Rem RECURSION IS IMPOSSIBLE, ON PURPOSE. A definition is validated
Rem against the dictionary as it stands, so its body may name only words
Rem that already exist and never the name being defined. The call graph
Rem is therefore acyclic and nesting depth is bounded by what was typed,
Rem which is how "nothing keyable aborts" survives contact with
Rem : LOOP LOOP ; — the definition is refused rather than the mill lost
Rem to a stack that never unwinds. Iteration is TIMES, MAP, FILTER and
Rem FOLD, which is what the combinators are for.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "cuttle.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "sinq.shoddy"
Include "str.shoddy"
Include "dict.shoddy"

Rem ---- the marks the tokenizer and the evaluator agree on -------------
Rem Ordinary tokens, separated by whitespace like everything else: there
Rem is no second grammar and no self-delimiting character, so { 1 2 3 }
Rem is four tokens and {1 2 3} is one word that does not exist.

Def RckDefMark() As String
    ":"

Def RckEndMark() As String
    ";"

Def RckListOpen() As String
    "{"

Def RckListClose() As String
    "}"

Def RckProgOpen() As String
    "["

Def RckProgClose() As String
    "]"

Def RckQuoteMark() As String
    Chr(34)

Rem The two angle modes, and the two group labels the dictionary starts
Rem out with. A seed names its own group through RckSeeding.

Def RckDegrees() As String
    "DEG"

Def RckRadians() As String
    "RAD"

Def RckCoreName() As String
    "core"

Def RckUserName() As String
    "user"

Rem Fifty states of history, oldest dropped. A cap rather than a growing
Rem tape because the tape is a convenience and a session is not a
Rem transaction log. The forward history REDO pops from is bounded by the
Rem same number, since it can never hold more than the history gave it.
Def RckHistCap() As Number
    50

Rem Five hundred rendered rows of tape, oldest dropped. Larger than the
Rem history because a row is a line of text and a history entry is a
Rem whole stack, and because the tape is what someone saves and reads
Rem afterwards rather than something the engine walks.
Def RckTapeCap() As Number
    500

Rem THE SESSION WORD WIDTH, in bits: how wide a machine word the bit
Rem operations pretend to be working in. A mode and not an argument,
Rem beside RckFmt and RckAngle, because it is a property of the session
Rem that several seeds read and not a fact about any one call. Bit words
Rem carrying a width argument each is what the seeds did before it
Rem existed, and forty of them would have been forty repetitions of a
Rem number nobody changes between one line and the next.
Rem
Rem 1 to 53. The ceiling is where a double stops counting consecutive
Rem whole numbers — 2 ^ 53 + 1 IS 2 ^ 53 — so a 54-bit word cannot
Rem represent its own top bit. That is an engine fact about Number and
Rem is why it is stated here rather than borrowed: bool.shoddy reaches
Rem the same 53 from the same arithmetic, and seedbooltest asserts the
Rem two agree rather than either including the other for it.
Def RckMaxWidth() As Number
    53

Rem Thirty-two, which is the width somebody means when they have not
Rem said. bool.shoddy's BoolBits() is the same number for the same
Rem reason; neither machine includes the other to get it.
Def RckWidthStart() As Number
    32

Rem How wide the WORDS listing packs its names. Nothing to do with
Rem RckMaxWidth above: this one is a column count for text.
Def RckListWidth() As Number
    68

Rem The trace's one measurement: the column its marker sits in. The token
Rem field shrinks as the indent grows so this column holds, which is what
Rem makes the stacks line up down a cascade that changes depth.
Def RckTraceBar() As Number
    14

Def RckTraceStep() As Number
    2

Rem ---- the types -------------------------------------------------------

Rem What a registered primitive answers: the cells it leaves, deepest
Rem first, or a refusal. Never an abort.
Rem RckOpened is the ONE state change a registered word may ask for, and
Rem it is deliberately not a state. A word answers VALUES, which is what
Rem keeps a seed from rewriting the dictionary, the registers or the tape
Rem on its way past — so "I have opened something, take it" cannot be
Rem expressed as "here is a new state" without handing every seed that
Rem power for the sake of one. Instead the word describes what it opened
Rem and the CORE binds it, which also keeps the table's invariants in the
Rem one file that owns them.
Rem
Rem The word must check RckBoundTo BEFORE it opens anything — that is
Rem what RckPureOver is for — because by the time this reaches the core
Rem the resource is already live. RckWorkDone shuts what it is handed
Rem rather than leaking it if the name turns out to be taken, but that is
Rem a backstop and not the design.
Rem RckUpdated is RckOpened's other half, and the resource model is not
Rem complete without it. A recio file, an isam table and a window all
Rem keep their state where the operating system does, so a word reads
Rem the binding and never writes it. A TURTLE does not: its position,
Rem heading and pen live in Shoddy, and every step changes them. Without
Rem a way to say "the thing under this name has moved", such a resource
Rem could be opened and read but never advanced.
Rem
Rem It carries VALUES as well, because a word that moves the turtle and
Rem answers where it is now is one word, not two.
Rem
Rem The name must already be bound — this REPLACES a binding's data and
Rem does not create one, so it cannot be used to smuggle a resource in
Rem without a closer.
Type RckWork = RckVals(WorkVals As List Of Cell) | RckWorkBad(WorkWhy As String) | RckOpened(OpenName As String, OpenKind As String, OpenHand As Number, OpenData As Cell, OpenShut As [ -- Boolean ]) | RckUpdated(UpName As String, UpData As Cell, UpVals As List Of Cell)

Rem WHAT A REGISTERED WORD IS. Five kinds, and the constructor a word is
Rem written with IS its effect declaration: a word cannot be registered
Rem without saying whether it touches the world. RckReg therefore keeps
Rem the six parameters the API states, and the flag rides where it cannot
Rem be forgotten.
Rem
Rem RckCore is the engine's own: dispatched by tag rather than by name,
Rem so an alias registered over one keeps working, and reaching the
Rem state is why they are not RckPure. They are all pure regardless —
Rem the engine performs no effect.
Rem
Rem RckPureOver is RckPure PLUS the state, and exists for one reason: a
Rem word whose argument is a [ ... ] program has to be able to RUN it,
Rem and running one needs the dictionary, the registers and the modes it
Rem was typed against. MAP, FILTER, FOLD and TIMES have always had that
Rem because they are RckCore and the core is dispatched with the state in
Rem hand; RckPureOver is the same reach, opened to a seed, so a machine
Rem whose words take a function — a numeric derivative, an integrator, a
Rem root finder — can be bridged at all. The word is still declared
Rem PURE, exactly as MAP is: what the program does is the program's, and
Rem a combinator answers for itself.
Type RckBody = RckCore(CoreTag As String) | RckPure(PureFn As [ List Of Cell -- RckWork ]) | RckTouch(TouchFn As [ List Of Cell -- RckWork ]) | RckPureOver(OverFn As [ RckState List Of Cell -- RckWork ]) | RckUser(UserTokens As List Of String, UserTouch As Boolean)

Rem A dictionary entry. The metadata is mandatory (R4.9) and the whole
Rem reason HELP and WORDS can answer at all; a word without it is
Rem undiscoverable at exactly the moment someone is exploring.
Type RckEntry
    WordName   As String
    WordArity  As Number
    WordBody   As RckBody
    WordEffect As String
    WordDesc   As String
    WordSeed   As String

Rem The whole of a session. Every keystroke produces a new one of these,
Rem which is why UNDO is a consequence of the semantics rather than a
Rem feature.
Rem
Rem The last three are appended rather than woven in, per the append-only
Rem rule. RckFore is what REDO pops: UNDO is destructive — it drops the
Rem two history entries it stepped over — so the stack it walked away
Rem from has to be banked somewhere or it is gone. RckTape and RckTrace
Rem live here rather than in a shell because TAPE, CLEARTAPE, TRACE and
Rem NOTRACE are dictionary words, and a dictionary word reaches the state
Rem or it reaches nothing.
Rem ---- a live resource, held by NAME ------------------------------------

Rem R3.5(b) says a handle must never reach the stack, and its ground is
Rem exactly this: a handle is a NUMBER, and UNDO restores the stack, so
Rem the number that named an open file would be gone while the file
Rem stayed open. That is a true account of a handle ON THE STACK. It is
Rem not an account of a resource held anywhere else.
Rem
Rem RckUndone rewrites RckStack, RckHist and RckFore, and nothing else —
Rem so a resource bound HERE, under a name the session chose, is still
Rem bound after any number of undos. The name is the whole of the
Rem difference: it is not a value that can be dropped, overwritten or
Rem undone away, and CLOSE and BOUND can always reach what is open
Rem because the table, not the stack, is where openness lives.
Rem
Rem THE CLOSER TRAVELS WITH THE RESOURCE. The core cannot know how to
Rem shut a recio file, an isam table or a plotter — it would have to
Rem depend on every machine that has one. So whatever opened it supplies
Rem a closer that has the handle closed over inside it, and the core
Rem shuts everything the same way without knowing what any of it is.
Rem This is the same shape isam.shoddy already uses for Rd/Wr/KeyOf.
Rem
Rem ResKind is for the session to read in BOUND, never for the core to
Rem branch on. The moment the core branches on a kind it is back to
Rem depending on the machines.
Rem
Rem ResHand AND ResData ARE THE MACHINE'S, and the core only carries
Rem them. A registered word is registered ONCE, long before anything is
Rem opened, so it cannot close over a handle the way seedisam's words
Rem close over an already-open table — it is handed a name at the prompt
Rem and has to find the resource from that. ResHand is what it opened
Rem (a file handle, a table handle) and ResData is how it should be read
Rem (recio's field layout, say), both put there by the machine's own
Rem OPEN word and meaningless to anything else.
Rem
Rem YES, THAT IS A HANDLE HELD IN THE STATE, and it is the point rather
Rem than a leak. R3.5(b) is about the STACK: a handle there is lost when
Rem UNDO restores the stack, and nothing can shut what nothing can name.
Rem Here it is reachable by name and unreachable by value — BOUND is
Rem built from ResName and ResKind alone, and no word anywhere pushes
Rem ResHand. That invariant is asserted in reckonertest.
Type RckRes
    ResName As String
    ResKind As String
    ResHand As Number
    ResData As Cell
    ResShut As [ -- Boolean ]

Type RckState
    RckStack As Stack
    RckDict  As List Of RckEntry
    RckRegs  As List Of Pair
    RckFmt   As CutFmt
    RckAngle As String
    RckHist  As List Of Stack
    RckLastX As Option
    RckSeed  As String
    RckDeep  As Number
    RckTrace As Boolean
    RckTape  As List Of String
    RckFore  As List Of Stack
    RckOpen  As List Of RckRes
    RckWidth As Number

Rem What evaluating a line answers. Both cases carry output lines for the
Rem shell to print, and the failing case carries the state as it was.
Type RckLine = RckNext(NextState As RckState, NextOut As List Of String) | RckStop(StopState As RckState, StopWhy As String, StopOut As List Of String)

Rem The same answer inside the evaluator, where a break carries no state
Rem because there is no state a caller may keep: the line is the
Rem transaction, and the state that stands is the one the line started
Rem from.
Type RckRun = RckWent(WentState As RckState, WentOut As List Of String) | RckBroke(BrokeWhy As String, BrokeOut As List Of String)

Rem Pulling a word's arguments off the stack.
Type RckTaken = RckHasArgs(ArgState As RckState, ArgVals As List Of Cell) | RckArgsBad(ArgWhy As String)

Rem Scanning to a closing mark: what was inside, and what came after.
Type RckSpan = RckSpanned(SpanIn As List Of String, SpanTail As List Of String) | RckSpanBad(SpanWhy As String)

Rem Whether a token list is a whole logical line, decided WITHOUT running
Rem it. RckSealed is complete, RckAjar is waiting for a closing mark that
Rem another line could still supply, RckWrecked can never close whatever
Rem is added. Only RckAjar means anything to a caller: a reader that has
Rem more input to offer gathers another line, and everything else is
Rem evaluated exactly as it is today.
Type RckShape = RckSealed | RckAjar(AjarOpen As String) | RckWrecked(WreckWhy As String)

Rem The accumulator the combinators fold with. Carrying the break in the
Rem accumulator rather than recursing is what keeps MAP over a
Rem six-hundred element list from being six hundred Shoddy frames deep.
Type RckFlow = RckFlowing(FlowState As RckState, FlowVals As List Of Cell, FlowOut As List Of String) | RckFlowBad(FlowWhy As String, FlowOut As List Of String)

Rem One display row: what a graphical shell tags a line with. RckShow is
Rem built from these, so the two renders cannot disagree.
Type RckRow
    RowLabel As String
    RowKind  As String
    RowText  As String

Rem ---- building a state ------------------------------------------------

Rem Empty of everything, RAD, STD, thirty-two bits. Radians because that
Rem is what the tree speaks everywhere else and PI 2 / SIN should work
Rem before anything is set; DEG is one word away and exists for entry,
Rem which is the only thing it exists for. The width starts where
Rem RckWidthStart says, so a session that never types WIDTH sees exactly
Rem what the bit words did before the mode existed.
Def RckBare() As RckState
    RckState(CutNew(), { }, { }, CutFmtStd(), RckRadians(), { }, None(), RckCoreName(), 0, False, { }, { }, { }, RckWidthStart())

Rem The engine core seeded. This is what a mill starts from, and what a
Rem seed file is handed.
Def RckNew() As RckState
    Fold(RckCoreWords(), RckBare(), RckPut)

Rem The group the next registrations land in. A seed opens with this and
Rem the whole file lands under one heading in WORDS; forgetting it is
Rem untidy rather than wrong, and puts the words under "core".
Def RckSeeding(st As RckState, label As String) As RckState
    With(st, RckSeed = label)

Rem ---- the dictionary --------------------------------------------------

Def RckPut(st As RckState, e As RckEntry) As RckState
    With(st, RckDict = Append(RckWithout(RckDict(st), WordName(e)), e))

Def RckWithout(d As List Of RckEntry, name As String) As List Of RckEntry
    Filter(d, Fn(e) => WordName(e) <> name)

Rem ---- the open-resource table -----------------------------------------

Rem The API a machine's own OPEN word uses once it has a live handle:
Rem bind it under the name the session gave, and hand over a closer with
Rem that handle closed over inside it. From then on the core owns
Rem shutting it, and the session owns the name.
Rem
Rem THESE TAKE A TYPED HELPER RATHER THAN REACHING INTO AN RckRes INSIDE
Rem A LAMBDA. Filter and Map pass their element as a generic t, and a
Rem record accessor cannot resolve against a type that is not yet known
Rem — it partially applies into a quotation instead and fails a long way
Rem from the mistake. ResNameOf is the typed door in.
Def ResNameOf(r As RckRes) As String
    ResName(r)

Def ResKindOf(r As RckRes) As String
    ResKind(r)

Def ResHandOf(r As RckRes) As Number
    ResHand(r)

Def ResDataOf(r As RckRes) As Cell
    ResData(r)

Def RckResOf(st As RckState, name As String) As List Of RckRes
    Filter(RckOpen(st), Fn(r) => ResNameOf(r) = name)

Def RckBoundTo(st As RckState, name As String) As Boolean
    Not IsEmpty(RckResOf(st, name))

Rem Binding over a name already bound would drop the old resource's
Rem closer on the floor — the leak this table exists to prevent, with
Rem an extra step. A caller checks RckBoundTo first and says so in its
Rem own words; this one appends, because the check belongs where the
Rem word's name can be put in the message.
Rem
Rem THE ONE THING THIS TABLE CANNOT CHECK is two names bound to the SAME
Rem handle. Closing both would shut it twice, and a second BClose on a
Rem handle already returned aborts. The core has no way to see it: a
Rem closer is an opaque function and comparing two of them is not a
Rem question Shoddy can ask. So it belongs to whatever opens the
Rem resource — an OPEN word hands out one binding per open, and opening
Rem the same path twice takes two handles, which is what the runtime
Rem does anyway.
Def RckBind(st As RckState, name As String, kind As String, hand As Number, data As Cell, shut As [ -- Boolean ]) As RckState
    With(st, RckOpen = Append(RckOpen(st), RckRes(name, kind, hand, data, shut)))

Def RckUnbind(st As RckState, name As String) As RckState
    With(st, RckOpen = Filter(RckOpen(st), Fn(r) => ResNameOf(r) <> name))

Def RckBoundNames(st As RckState) As List Of String
    Map(RckOpen(st), ResNameOf)

Rem Calling the closer and keeping the state. The If has the same answer
Rem either way on purpose: whether the resource reported a clean shut is
Rem the CALLER's to report, and this one exists only to make the call
Rem happen in a position where its answer can be looked at.
Def RckShutting(r As RckRes) As Boolean
    Call(ResShut(r))

Rem The registration API. Names fold to UPPER here and at lookup, as the
Rem language itself does; the seed group comes from the state so the six
Rem parameters stay the six the API states.
Def RckReg(st As RckState, name As String, arity As Number, body As RckBody, effect As String, desc As String) As RckState
    RckPut(st, RckEntry(Upper(name), arity, body, effect, desc, RckSeed(st)))

Def RckUnreg(st As RckState, name As String) As RckState
    With(st, RckDict = RckWithout(RckDict(st), Upper(name)))

Rem Option, not an entry-or-sentinel: a dictionary miss is an ordinary
Rem answer and the caller has to look at it.
Def RckFind(st As RckState, name As String) As Option
    FindOpt(RckDict(st), Fn(e) => WordName(e) = Upper(name))

Def RckKnows(st As RckState, name As String) As Boolean
    Select Case RckFind(st, name)
        Case Some(e)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Rem Whether a word touches the world. A user word's flag is the union of
Rem the words its body names, worked out once at definition time — which
Rem is what lets someone auditing a downloaded program see in one place
Rem what it will do.
Def RckTouches(e As RckEntry) As Boolean
    Select Case WordBody(e)
        Case RckTouch(f)
            True
        Case RckUser(ts, t)
            t
        Case Else
            False

Def RckIsUser(e As RckEntry) As Boolean
    Select Case WordBody(e)
        Case RckUser(ts, t)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Def RckTokensOf(e As RckEntry) As List Of String
    Select Case WordBody(e)
        Case RckUser(ts, t)
            ts
        Case Else
            { }

Rem ---- the tokenizer ---------------------------------------------------
Rem Whitespace splits; a "..." string is one token and may hold spaces.
Rem The quotes are KEPT in the token, so a token is exactly the text that
Rem produced it: a saved definition re-reads to the same tokens, and
Rem VIEW prints back what was typed. There are no escapes inside a
Rem string — the first closing quote ends it — because a calculator
Rem entry line is not a source file.

Def RckTokens(line As String) As Result
    RckScan(line, 1, "", { })

Def RckScan(s As String, i As Number, cur As String, acc As List Of String) As Result
    If i > Len(s) Then
        Ok(RckFlush(cur, acc))
    Else
        If Mid(s, i, 1) = RckQuoteMark() Then
            RckScanText(s, i + 1, "", RckFlush(cur, acc))
        Else
            If Asc(Mid(s, i, 1)) <= 32 Then
                RckScan(s, i + 1, "", RckFlush(cur, acc))
            Else
                RckScan(s, i + 1, cur & Mid(s, i, 1), acc)

Def RckScanText(s As String, i As Number, cur As String, acc As List Of String) As Result
    If i > Len(s) Then
        Err("unterminated string, there is no closing " & RckQuoteMark(), 0)
    Else
        If Mid(s, i, 1) = RckQuoteMark() Then
            RckScan(s, i + 1, "", Append(acc, RckQuoteMark() & cur & RckQuoteMark()))
        Else
            RckScanText(s, i + 1, cur & Mid(s, i, 1), acc)

Def RckFlush(cur As String, acc As List Of String) As List Of String
    If cur = "" Then
        acc
    Else
        Append(acc, cur)

Def RckIsText(t As String) As Boolean
    Len(t) >= 2 And Left(t, 1) = RckQuoteMark() And Right(t, 1) = RckQuoteMark()

Def RckTextOf(t As String) As String
    Mid(t, 2, Len(t) - 2)

Rem ---- reading an argument ---------------------------------------------
Rem Result, because these answer a question that has a reason: the caller
Rem turns Err into a refusal and the whole line stands down. Every one of
Rem them names the kind it got, since "MEAN needs a LIST, got STRING" is
Rem the message that tells someone what they typed wrong.

Def RckKindWhy(word As String, wanted As String, c As Cell) As String
    word & " needs " & wanted & ", got " & CutKindOf(c)

Def RckNumArg(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutNum(x)
            Ok(x)
        Case Else
            Err(RckKindWhy(word, "a NUMBER", c), 0)

Def RckWholeArg(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutNum(x)
            If x < 0 Or x <> Floor(x) Then
                Err(word & " needs a whole number of 0 or more, got " & Str(x), 0)
            Else
                Ok(x)
        Case Else
            Err(RckKindWhy(word, "a NUMBER", c), 0)

Def RckTextArg(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutStr(t)
            Ok(t)
        Case Else
            Err(RckKindWhy(word, "a STRING", c), 0)

Def RckBoolArg(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutBool(b)
            Ok(b)
        Case Else
            Err(RckKindWhy(word, "a BOOLEAN", c), 0)

Def RckListArg(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutList(xs)
            Ok(xs)
        Case Else
            Err(RckKindWhy(word, "a LIST", c), 0)

Def RckProgArg(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutProg(ts)
            Ok(ts)
        Case Else
            Err(RckKindWhy(word, "a PROGRAM", c), 0)

Rem ---- lifting a plain function into a word ----------------------------
Rem The guard is a parameter rather than a line inside each word, so a
Rem domain rule is written once, beside the word it belongs to, and the
Rem refusal reads in the same shape everywhere. Every builtin that would
Rem Error on a bad domain is reached through one of these.

Def RckAlways(x As Number) As Boolean
    True

Def RckAlways2(a As Number, b As Number) As Boolean
    True

Def RckNonZero(a As Number, b As Number) As Boolean
    b <> 0

Rem Math.Pow raises on a result that is not finite, and answers NaN for a
Rem negative base under a fractional power, so both are refused before it
Rem is reached. The magnitude test is in logarithms because that is the
Rem only way to ask "would this overflow" without overflowing.
Def RckPowOk(a As Number, b As Number) As Boolean
    If a = 0 Then
        b >= 0
    Else
        If a < 0 And b <> Floor(b) Then
            False
        Else
            Abs(b) * Log10(Abs(a)) < 300

Def RckUnit(x As Number) As Boolean
    x >= 0 - 1 And x <= 1

Def RckLift1(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, fits As [ Number -- Boolean ], why As String, f As [ Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Select Case First(vs)
        Case CutNum(x)
            If Call(fits, x) Then
                RckVals({ CutNum(Call(f, x)) })
            Else
                RckWorkBad(word & " " & why)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(RckKindWhy(word, "a NUMBER", First(vs)))

Def RckLift2(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, fits As [ Number Number -- Boolean ], why As String, f As [ Number Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Select Case Nth(vs, 1)
        Case CutNum(a)
            Select Case Nth(vs, 2)
                Case CutNum(b)
                    If Call(fits, a, b) Then
                        RckVals({ CutNum(Call(f, a, b)) })
                    Else
                        RckWorkBad(word & " " & why)
                Case Else
                    RckWorkBad(RckKindWhy(word, "two NUMBERs", Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(RckKindWhy(word, "two NUMBERs", Nth(vs, 1)))

Rem cuttle's arithmetic already answers rather than aborting; this is the
Rem one line that turns its answer into a word's answer.
Def RckCalc(c As CutCalc) As RckWork
    Select Case c
        Case CutGot(v)
            RckVals({ v })
        Case CutCalcBad(w)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("the arithmetic did not answer")

Rem One ordering word underneath four spellings: > is < with the
Rem arguments the other way round, and <= and >= are their negations.
Def RckOrder(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, flip As Boolean, negate As Boolean) As RckWork
    If flip Then
        RckOrdered(word, CutLess(Nth(vs, 2), Nth(vs, 1)), negate)
    Else
        RckOrdered(word, CutLess(Nth(vs, 1), Nth(vs, 2)), negate)

Def RckOrdered(word As String, c As CutCalc, negate As Boolean) As RckWork
    Select Case c
        Case CutGot(v)
            Select Case v
                Case CutBool(p)
                    RckVals({ CutBool(Ifte(negate, Not p, p)) })
                Case Else
                    RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer with a BOOLEAN")
        Case CutCalcBad(w)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def RckBool1(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ Boolean -- Boolean ]) As RckWork
    Select Case RckBoolArg(word, First(vs))
        Case Ok(p)
            RckVals({ CutBool(Call(f, p)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def RckBool2(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ Boolean Boolean -- Boolean ]) As RckWork
    Select Case RckBoolArg(word, Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(p)
            RckBool2On(word, p, Nth(vs, 2), f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def RckBool2On(word As String, p As Boolean, c As Cell, f As [ Boolean Boolean -- Boolean ]) As RckWork
    Select Case RckBoolArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(q)
            RckVals({ CutBool(Call(f, p, q)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def RckJust(c As Cell) As RckWork
    RckVals({ c })

Rem ---- the engine core -------------------------------------------------
Rem The words every mill gets before it seeds anything, in one table so
Rem that "every entry appears exactly once" is a thing that can be read
Rem rather than believed. Arithmetic, comparison and logic are ordinary
Rem registered primitives — they need nothing but their arguments. The
Rem rest are RckCore, dispatched by tag in RckCoreDo, because they reach
Rem the stack, the registers, the modes, the history or the evaluator
Rem itself, and none of those is an argument.
Rem
Rem What is deliberately NOT here: RANGE, LENGTH, CONCAT and the rest of
Rem the sequence words, which belong to seed-seq; the trigonometry that
Rem does not honour the angle mode; and anything that touches the world.

Def RckCoreOf(name As String, arity As Number, body As RckBody, effect As String, desc As String) As RckEntry
    RckEntry(name, arity, body, effect, desc, RckCoreName())

Def RckCoreWords() As List Of RckEntry
    Concat(RckCoreMath(), Concat(RckCoreTest(), Concat(RckCoreStack(), RckCoreRest())))

Def RckCoreMath() As List Of RckEntry
    { RckCoreOf("+", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckCalc(CutAdd(Nth(vs, 1), Nth(vs, 2)))), "( a b -- a+b )", "Add two cells: numbers, money, matrices of one shape, or strings joined."),
      RckCoreOf("-", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckCalc(CutSub(Nth(vs, 1), Nth(vs, 2)))), "( a b -- a-b )", "Subtract: numbers, or money exact at the cent."),
      RckCoreOf("*", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckCalc(CutMul(Nth(vs, 1), Nth(vs, 2)))), "( a b -- a*b )", "Multiply: numbers, matrices, or a matrix scaled by a number."),
      RckCoreOf("/", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift2("/", vs, RckNonZero, "cannot divide by zero", /)), "( a b -- a/b )", "Divide one number by another."),
      RckCoreOf("^", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift2("^", vs, RckPowOk, "cannot raise that base to that power", ^)), "( a b -- a^b )", "Raise a number to a power."),
      RckCoreOf("MOD", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift2("MOD", vs, RckNonZero, "cannot take a remainder by zero", Mod)), "( a b -- r )", "Truncated remainder, which keeps the sign of a."),
      RckCoreOf("WRAP", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift2("WRAP", vs, RckNonZero, "cannot wrap by zero", Wrap)), "( a b -- r )", "Floored modulo: the answer keeps the sign of b, so -340 256 WRAP is 172."),
      RckCoreOf("ABS", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift1("ABS", vs, RckAlways, "", Abs)), "( x -- |x| )", "Magnitude, without the sign."),
      RckCoreOf("SGN", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift1("SGN", vs, RckAlways, "", Sgn)), "( x -- s )", "The sign of a number: -1, 0 or 1."),
      RckCoreOf("FLOOR", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift1("FLOOR", vs, RckAlways, "", Floor)), "( x -- n )", "The largest whole number no greater than x."),
      RckCoreOf("CEIL", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift1("CEIL", vs, RckAlways, "", Ceil)), "( x -- n )", "The smallest whole number no less than x."),
      RckCoreOf("ROUND", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift1("ROUND", vs, RckAlways, "", Round)), "( x -- n )", "Round to the nearest whole number, halves upward."),
      RckCoreOf("MIN", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift2("MIN", vs, RckAlways2, "", Min)), "( a b -- m )", "The smaller of two numbers."),
      RckCoreOf("MAX", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift2("MAX", vs, RckAlways2, "", Max)), "( a b -- m )", "The larger of two numbers."),
      RckCoreOf("SQRT", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift1("SQRT", vs, >=(0), "has no answer below zero", Sqr)), "( x -- root )", "Square root; refuses a negative number."),
      RckCoreOf("EXP", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift1("EXP", vs, <=(709), "would overflow above 709", Exp)), "( x -- e^x )", "The exponential of x."),
      RckCoreOf("LN", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift1("LN", vs, >(0), "has no answer at or below zero", Log)), "( x -- ln x )", "Natural logarithm; refuses zero and below."),
      RckCoreOf("LOG", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckLift1("LOG", vs, >(0), "has no answer at or below zero", Log10)), "( x -- log x )", "Logarithm base ten; refuses zero and below."),
      RckCoreOf("PI", 0, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckJust(CutNum(Pi()))), "( -- pi )", "The ratio of a circle to its diameter."),
      RckCoreOf("E", 0, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckJust(CutNum(Exp(1)))), "( -- e )", "The base of the natural logarithm.") }

Def RckCoreTest() As List Of RckEntry
    { RckCoreOf("=", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckJust(CutBool(CutEqual(Nth(vs, 1), Nth(vs, 2))))), "( a b -- flag )", "Whether two cells are equal, all the way down."),
      RckCoreOf("<>", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckJust(CutBool(Not CutEqual(Nth(vs, 1), Nth(vs, 2))))), "( a b -- flag )", "Whether two cells differ."),
      RckCoreOf("<", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckOrder("<", vs, False, False)), "( a b -- flag )", "Whether a orders before b; numbers, strings and money order."),
      RckCoreOf(">", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckOrder(">", vs, True, False)), "( a b -- flag )", "Whether a orders after b."),
      RckCoreOf("<=", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckOrder("<=", vs, True, True)), "( a b -- flag )", "Whether a does not order after b."),
      RckCoreOf(">=", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckOrder(">=", vs, False, True)), "( a b -- flag )", "Whether a does not order before b."),
      RckCoreOf("NOT", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool1("NOT", vs, Fn(p) => Not p)), "( flag -- flag )", "The other truth value."),
      RckCoreOf("AND", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("AND", vs, Fn(p, q) => p And q)), "( a b -- flag )", "True when both are true."),
      RckCoreOf("OR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("OR", vs, Fn(p, q) => p Or q)), "( a b -- flag )", "True when either is true."),
      RckCoreOf("TRUE", 0, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckJust(CutBool(True))), "( -- flag )", "The true value."),
      RckCoreOf("FALSE", 0, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckJust(CutBool(False))), "( -- flag )", "The false value.") }

Def RckCoreStack() As List Of RckEntry
    { RckCoreOf("DUP", 0, RckCore("DUP"), "( x -- x x )", "Copy the top cell."),
      RckCoreOf("DROP", 0, RckCore("DROP"), "( x -- )", "Discard the top cell."),
      RckCoreOf("SWAP", 0, RckCore("SWAP"), "( x y -- y x )", "Exchange the top two cells."),
      RckCoreOf("OVER", 0, RckCore("OVER"), "( x y -- x y x )", "Copy the second cell over the top."),
      RckCoreOf("NIP", 0, RckCore("NIP"), "( x y -- y )", "Discard the second cell."),
      RckCoreOf("TUCK", 0, RckCore("TUCK"), "( x y -- y x y )", "Copy the top cell under the second."),
      RckCoreOf("ROT", 0, RckCore("ROT"), "( x y z -- y z x )", "Bring the third cell to the top."),
      RckCoreOf("ROLL", 1, RckCore("ROLL"), "( n -- )", "Move the cell at level n to the top, counting from 1 at the top."),
      RckCoreOf("PICK", 1, RckCore("PICK"), "( n -- x )", "Copy the cell at level n to the top."),
      RckCoreOf("DEPTH", 0, RckCore("DEPTH"), "( -- n )", "How many cells the stack holds."),
      RckCoreOf("CLEAR", 0, RckCore("CLEAR"), "( ... -- )", "Empty the stack."),
      RckCoreOf("GATHER", 1, RckCore("GATHER"), "( x1 .. xn n -- list )", "Collect the n cells under the count into one list."),
      RckCoreOf("UNPACK", 1, RckCore("UNPACK"), "( list -- x1 .. xn n )", "Spread a list onto the stack and push how many; UNPACK GATHER round-trips."),
      RckCoreOf("LASTX", 0, RckCore("LASTX"), "( -- x )", "The top cell the last word to take one consumed.") }

Def RckCoreRest() As List Of RckEntry
    { RckCoreOf("STO", 2, RckCore("STO"), "( x name -- )", "Bank a cell in a named register."),
      RckCoreOf("RCL", 1, RckCore("RCL"), "( name -- x )", "Fetch a cell from a named register."),
      RckCoreOf("CLOSE", 1, RckCore("CLOSE"), "( name -- )", "Shut the resource opened under that name and let the name go."),
      RckCoreOf("CLOSEALL", 0, RckCore("CLOSEALL"), "( -- n )", "Shut everything that is open and push how many there were."),
      RckCoreOf("BOUND", 0, RckCore("BOUND"), "( -- list )", "What is open right now: a list of name/kind pairs, empty when nothing is."),
      RckCoreOf("UNDO", 0, RckCore("UNDO"), "( -- )", "Put the stack back as the previous line found it."),
      RckCoreOf("REDO", 0, RckCore("REDO"), "( -- )", "Put back the stack UNDO stepped away from; anything else you do clears it."),
      RckCoreOf("TRACE", 0, RckCore("TRACE"), "( -- )", "Show the stack after every token, indented where a program runs, until NOTRACE."),
      RckCoreOf("NOTRACE", 0, RckCore("NOTRACE"), "( -- )", "Stop showing the stack after every token."),
      RckCoreOf("TAPE", 0, RckCore("TAPE"), "( -- n )", "How many rows the tape holds; it keeps the last " & Str(RckTapeCap()) & " and drops the oldest."),
      RckCoreOf("CLEARTAPE", 0, RckCore("CLEARTAPE"), "( -- )", "Throw the tape away and start it again empty."),
      RckCoreOf("DEG", 0, RckCore("DEG"), "( -- )", "Read and write angles in degrees."),
      RckCoreOf("RAD", 0, RckCore("RAD"), "( -- )", "Read and write angles in radians."),
      RckCoreOf("FIX", 1, RckCore("FIX"), "( n -- )", "Show numbers to n decimal places."),
      RckCoreOf("SCI", 1, RckCore("SCI"), "( n -- )", "Show numbers in scientific notation to n places."),
      RckCoreOf("ENG", 1, RckCore("ENG"), "( n -- )", "Show numbers in engineering notation to n places."),
      RckCoreOf("STD", 0, RckCore("STD"), "( -- )", "Show numbers at their own precision."),
      RckCoreOf("WIDTH", 1, RckCore("WIDTH"), "( n -- )", "Work the bit words in an n-bit word, 1 to " & Str(RckMaxWidth()) & ". It does not change how numbers are shown."),
      RckCoreOf("WIDTH?", 0, RckCore("WIDTH?"), "( -- n )", "How wide the bit words think the word is; WIDTH sets it and it starts at " & Str(RckWidthStart()) & "."),
      RckCoreOf("SIN", 1, RckCore("SIN"), "( a -- x )", "Sine of an angle, in the current angle mode."),
      RckCoreOf("COS", 1, RckCore("COS"), "( a -- x )", "Cosine of an angle, in the current angle mode."),
      RckCoreOf("TAN", 1, RckCore("TAN"), "( a -- x )", "Tangent of an angle, in the current angle mode."),
      RckCoreOf("ASIN", 1, RckCore("ASIN"), "( x -- a )", "The angle whose sine is x; refuses x outside -1 to 1."),
      RckCoreOf("ACOS", 1, RckCore("ACOS"), "( x -- a )", "The angle whose cosine is x; refuses x outside -1 to 1."),
      RckCoreOf("ATAN", 1, RckCore("ATAN"), "( x -- a )", "The angle whose tangent is x."),
      RckCoreOf("ATAN2", 2, RckCore("ATAN2"), "( y x -- a )", "The angle of the point x y from the origin, in all four quadrants."),
      RckCoreOf("IFT", 3, RckCore("IFT"), "( x flag prog -- y )", "Run prog on x when flag is true, otherwise leave x."),
      RckCoreOf("IFTE", 4, RckCore("IFTE"), "( x flag then else -- y )", "Run one of two programs on x, chosen by flag."),
      RckCoreOf("TIMES", 3, RckCore("TIMES"), "( x prog n -- y )", "Run prog on x n times over, each pass taking the last answer."),
      RckCoreOf("MAP", 2, RckCore("MAP"), "( list prog -- list )", "Run prog on every element and collect the answers."),
      RckCoreOf("FILTER", 2, RckCore("FILTER"), "( list prog -- list )", "Keep the elements prog answers True for."),
      RckCoreOf("FOLD", 3, RckCore("FOLD"), "( list init prog -- x )", "Run prog over each element with a running answer, starting from init."),
      RckCoreOf("HELP", 0, RckCore("HELP"), "( -- )", "Show what the word named next does; HELP MEAN, not " & Chr(34) & "MEAN" & Chr(34) & " HELP."),
      RckCoreOf("VIEW", 0, RckCore("VIEW"), "( -- )", "Print the word named next back as it was defined."),
      RckCoreOf("WORDS", 0, RckCore("WORDS"), "( -- )", "List every word in the dictionary, grouped by where it came from.") }

Rem ---- the two renders, one truth --------------------------------------
Rem RckRows is the render; RckShow is RckRows with the labels glued on.
Rem Written the other way round they could disagree, and a shell would
Rem have no way to tell which was right.

Def RckLabel(k As Number) As String
    Select Case k
        Case 1
            "x"
        Case 2
            "y"
        Case 3
            "z"
        Case 4
            "t"
        Case Else
            Str(k)

Def RckRows(st As RckState) As List Of RckRow
    RckRowsOf(CutCells(RckStack(st)), RckFmt(st))

Rem Deepest first, so the top level is the LAST line printed and a shell
Rem that appends to a scrolling terminal shows x nearest the prompt.
Def RckRowsOf(cells As List Of Cell, f As CutFmt) As List Of RckRow
    Map(Range(1, Length(cells)), Fn(i) => RckRowAt(cells, Length(cells) + 1 - i, f))

Def RckRowAt(cells As List Of Cell, k As Number, f As CutFmt) As RckRow
    RckRowOf(Nth(cells, k), RckLabel(k), f)

Def RckRowOf(c As Cell, label As String, f As CutFmt) As RckRow
    RckRow(label, CutKindOf(c), CutShow(c, f))

Rem RckShow is what a scrolling shell prints and what the tape records,
Rem so it is the render that has to be complete on its own: an empty
Rem stack says so rather than printing nothing, and a deep one names its
Rem depth rather than running down the terminal. Four levels because
Rem x/y/z/t are the four a stack machine has names for.
Rem
Rem RckRows is NOT capped and gains neither line. A graphical shell paints
Rem every row it has room for and puts the depth in a status field, which
Rem is a different arrangement of the same truth rather than a second
Rem truth — the rows themselves still come from one place.
Def RckShowCap() As Number
    4

Def RckShow(st As RckState) As List Of String
    RckShown(RckRows(st))

Def RckShown(rows As List Of RckRow) As List Of String
    If IsEmpty(rows) Then
        { "[ empty ]" }
    Else
        If Length(rows) <= RckShowCap() Then
            RckLabelled(rows)
        Else
            Prepend("[ depth " & Str(Length(rows)) & " ]", RckLabelled(RckTopRows(rows)))

Rem The rows arrive deepest first, so the shallowest RckShowCap of them
Rem are the LAST ones — and the marker goes above them, at the deep end,
Rem which is where the rows it is counting for would have been.
Def RckTopRows(rows As List Of RckRow) As List Of RckRow
    DropN(rows, Length(rows) - RckShowCap())

Def RckLabelled(rows As List Of RckRow) As List Of String
    Map(rows, Fn(r) => RowLabel(r) & ": " & RowText(r))

Rem ---- help and the word list ------------------------------------------

Def RckHelp(st As RckState, name As String) As List Of String
    Select Case RckFind(st, name)
        Case Some(e)
            RckHelpOf(e)
        Case Else
            { Upper(name) & " is not a word in this dictionary" }

Def RckHelpOf(e As RckEntry) As List Of String
    { WordName(e) & "  " & WordEffect(e), "    " & WordDesc(e), "    " & RckTouchLine(e) }

Def RckTouchLine(e As RckEntry) As String
    If RckTouches(e) Then
        "touches the world"
    Else
        "touches nothing"

Def RckView(st As RckState, name As String) As List Of String
    Select Case RckFind(st, name)
        Case Some(e)
            RckViewOf(e)
        Case Else
            { Upper(name) & " is not a word in this dictionary" }

Def RckViewOf(e As RckEntry) As List Of String
    If RckIsUser(e) Then
        { RckDefLine(e) }
    Else
        { WordName(e) & "  " & WordEffect(e), "    a built-in word: there is no program to view" }

Def RckDefLine(e As RckEntry) As String
    RckDefMark() & " " & WordName(e) & " " & Join(RckTokensOf(e), " ") & " " & RckEndMark()

Rem Grouped by seed, in the order the seeds arrived, because that is the
Rem order someone built the dictionary in and the order they will look
Rem for a word in.
Rem GroupBy answers its groups in first-appearance order, which IS the
Rem order the seeds arrived, so the heading order this promises falls out
Rem of it rather than being arranged. This was three Defs — a distinct
Rem over the seed names, then a rescan of the whole dictionary per name —
Rem before sinq had the word for it.
Def RckWords(st As RckState) As List Of String
    SelectMany(GroupBy(RckDict(st), WordSeed), RckGroupOf)

Def RckGroupOf(g As Group) As List Of String
    RckHeaded("-- " & GKey(g) & " --", Map(GItems(g), WordName))

Rem The other grouping the same listing supports: what a program will do
Rem to the world, in one place, without reading every word's help.
Def RckWordsEffect(st As RckState) As List Of String
    Concat(RckHeaded("-- touches nothing --", RckNamesIn(RckDict(st), Fn(e) => Not RckTouches(e))),
           RckHeaded("-- touches the world --", RckNamesIn(RckDict(st), RckTouches)))

Def RckNamesIn(d As List Of RckEntry, p As [ RckEntry -- Boolean ]) As List Of String
    Map(Filter(d, p), WordName)

Def RckHeaded(head As String, names As List Of String) As List Of String
    Prepend(head, RckPacked(names, ""))

Rem Greedy packing to RckListWidth columns: a dictionary of two hundred
Rem words is a wall of text one name per line and a paragraph packed.
Def RckPacked(names As List Of String, cur As String) As List Of String
    If IsEmpty(names) Then
        RckLastLine(cur)
    Else
        If cur = "" Then
            RckPacked(Rest(names), First(names))
        Else
            If Len(cur) + 1 + Len(First(names)) > RckListWidth() Then
                Prepend("  " & cur, RckPacked(names, ""))
            Else
                RckPacked(Rest(names), cur & " " & First(names))

Def RckLastLine(cur As String) As List Of String
    If cur = "" Then
        { }
    Else
        { "  " & cur }

Rem ---- the trace -------------------------------------------------------
Rem One row per token, and the row is a value like everything else: it
Rem goes onto the output lines the shell prints, so TRACE costs the engine
Rem no effect and no terminal.
Rem
Rem THE INDENT IS NOT DECORATION. A user word's body, a { } literal and a
Rem combinator's program all run on a FRESH stack, so the cells on an
Rem indented row are a different stack from the ones on the row above it.
Rem Printed flat, the cascade would read as one stack that suddenly lost
Rem its lower half. Two spaces per level of RckDeep is what says otherwise,
Rem and the » and « rows are where the change of stack happens.
Rem
Rem Trace rows deliberately do NOT go through RckSaid. That suppresses
Rem output below the entry line, which is right for an "ok:" nobody asked
Rem for and exactly wrong for a trace: the sub-evaluation RckSaid hides is
Rem the thing someone turned TRACE on to see.

Def RckTraceMark() As String
    "|"

Def RckTraceIn() As String
    "»"

Def RckTraceOut() As String
    "«"

Rem The field the token sits in shrinks as the indent grows, so the marker
Rem holds its column. A token too long for what is left is not cut short —
Rem the marker gives way for that row instead, because a trace that
Rem truncates the token is a trace of something else.
Def RckTraceRow(deep As Number, t As String, mark As String, text As String) As String
    Let pad = RckTraceStep() * (deep + 1)
    Let wide = Max(RckTraceBar() - 1 - pad, 1)
    StrRep(" ", pad) & PadRight(t, Max(wide, Len(t) + 1)) & mark & " " & text

Rem A stack as a trace row shows it: deepest first, top-rightmost, the
Rem same order and the same CutShow the labelled render uses. CutCells
Rem hands them back top first, hence the Reverse.
Def RckTraceStack(st As RckState) As String
    If CutDepth(RckStack(st)) = 0 Then
        "[ empty ]"
    Else
        Join(Reverse(Map(CutCells(RckStack(st)), Fn(c) => CutShow(c, RckFmt(st)))), " ")

Rem Tracing is read off the state BEFORE and AFTER, and both must agree,
Rem so TRACE does not trace itself turning on and NOTRACE does not trace
Rem itself turning off. Either would be a row about the trace rather than
Rem about the calculation.
Def RckTracing(st0 As RckState, st2 As RckState) As Boolean
    RckTrace(st0) And RckTrace(st2)

Rem A token that ran: the stack it left, at the caller's indent.
Def RckTraceDid(st0 As RckState, t As String, st2 As RckState, out As List Of String) As List Of String
    If RckTracing(st0, st2) Then
        Append(out, RckTraceRow(RckDeep(st0), t, RckTraceMark(), RckTraceStack(st2)))
    Else
        out

Rem Entering a sub-evaluation: the tokens about to run, where the stack
Rem would otherwise be. A combinator emits one of these per pass, which is
Rem what makes a MAP over three elements read as three runs of one program.
Def RckTraceInto(st As RckState, name As String, toks As List Of String, out As List Of String) As List Of String
    If RckTrace(st) Then
        Append(out, RckTraceRow(RckDeep(st), name, RckTraceIn(), Join(toks, " ")))
    Else
        out

Rem Leaving one, once its answer has been merged into the caller: the
Rem caller's stack, at the caller's indent. One per token however many
Rem passes ran, because one token is what returned.
Def RckTraceBack(st As RckState, name As String, st3 As RckState, out As List Of String) As List Of String
    If RckTracing(st, st3) Then
        Append(out, RckTraceRow(RckDeep(st), name, RckTraceOut(), RckTraceStack(st3)))
    Else
        out

Def RckBackFrom(st As RckState, name As String, st3 As RckState, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    RckWent(st3, RckTraceBack(st, name, st3, out))

Rem The tags whose own row is a « rather than a |, because they run a
Rem program and RckTraceBack has already reported them coming back. A
Rem second row from RckDidWord would say the same thing twice.
Def RckRunsProg(tag As String) As Boolean
    Contains({ "IFT", "IFTE", "TIMES", "MAP", "FILTER", "FOLD" }, tag)

Rem ---- the stack, as the evaluator moves it ----------------------------

Rem Any ordinary step forward abandons the forward history: the user has
Rem moved on, and a REDO into a branch they left would put back a stack
Rem that does not follow from what is on screen. UNDO and REDO are the two
Rem exceptions, being the words that own it. Guarding on empty keeps this
Rem free for every session that never undoes anything, which is most.
Def RckMoved(st As RckState) As RckState
    If IsEmpty(RckFore(st)) Then
        st
    Else
        With(st, RckFore = { })

Def RckPushed(st As RckState, c As Cell) As RckState
    With(RckMoved(st), RckStack = CutPush(RckStack(st), c))

Rem A literal is a token too, and gets its row like any other.
Def RckPushedOn(st As RckState, t As String, c As Cell, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    RckWalkedOn(st, t, RckPushed(st, c), more, out)

Def RckWalkedOn(st As RckState, t As String, st2 As RckState, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    RckWalk(st2, more, RckTraceDid(st, t, st2, out))

Def RckPushAll(st As RckState, cells As List Of Cell) As RckState
    With(st, RckStack = Fold(cells, RckStack(st), CutPush))

Rem A sub-evaluation's answer, brought home: everything the inner state
Rem learned is kept, and the stack goes back to the caller's with the
Rem cells handed over pushed onto it.
Def RckKeep(outer As RckState, inner As RckState, cells As List Of Cell) As RckState
    With(inner, RckStack = Fold(cells, RckStack(outer), CutPush), RckDeep = RckDeep(outer))

Def RckSameBut(outer As RckState, inner As RckState) As RckState
    With(inner, RckStack = RckStack(outer), RckDeep = RckDeep(outer))

Rem Pull a word's arguments off, deepest first, and remember the top one
Rem as LastX. Every word with an arity of one or more sets it, which is
Rem the one rule that makes LASTX predictable: 3 4 + leaves 4 there.
Def RckArgsOf(st As RckState, e As RckEntry) As RckTaken
    RckArgsAt(st, WordName(e), WordArity(e))

Def RckArgsAt(st As RckState, word As String, n As Number) As RckTaken
    If CutDepth(RckStack(st)) < n Then
        RckArgsBad(CutShort(word, n, CutDepth(RckStack(st))))
    Else
        RckHasArgs(RckMarked(With(st, RckStack = Stack(DropN(CutCells(RckStack(st)), n))), CutCells(RckStack(st)), n), Reverse(Taken(CutCells(RckStack(st)), n)))

Def RckMarked(st As RckState, cells As List Of Cell, n As Number) As RckState
    If n < 1 Then
        st
    Else
        With(st, RckLastX = Some(First(cells)))

Rem ---- scanning to a closing mark --------------------------------------

Def RckUpTo(toks As List Of String, open As String, close As String) As RckSpan
    RckUpFrom(toks, open, close, 0, { })

Def RckUpFrom(toks As List Of String, open As String, close As String, deep As Number, acc As List Of String) As RckSpan
    If IsEmpty(toks) Then
        RckSpanBad("unterminated " & open)
    Else
        If First(toks) = close And deep = 0 Then
            RckSpanned(Reverse(acc), Rest(toks))
        Else
            RckUpFrom(Rest(toks), open, close, deep + RckNesting(First(toks), open, close), Prepend(First(toks), acc))

Def RckNesting(t As String, open As String, close As String) As Number
    If t = open Then
        1
    Else
        If t = close Then
            0 - 1
        Else
            0

Def RckCloseOf(open As String) As String
    If open = RckListOpen() Then
        RckListClose()
    Else
        RckProgClose()

Rem ---- is this line finished? ------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem The same walk RckWalkOne does, with the state and the effects taken
Rem out: only the marks are looked at, so the answer costs a fold over the
Rem tokens and changes nothing. That is the whole point. A reader that
Rem asked "is this finished?" by RUNNING the line would have to run it
Rem again once the rest arrived, and a line that appended to a file or
Rem drew a random number would do it twice. Deciding on the tokens alone
Rem is what lets a definition be typed over several lines without the
Rem first line's words happening more than once.
Rem
Rem RckUpTo counts ONE pair of marks and knows nothing of the others, so
Rem ; closes a definition even with a { still open inside it. This
Rem inherits that exactly and then grades the body it extracted, which is
Rem what makes : FOO { 1 2 ; wrecked rather than waiting: the definition
Rem has shut, so no later line can reach the list. A construct that is
Rem still OPEN is not graded inside, because both layers can still close.

Def RckShapeOf(toks As List Of String) As RckShape
    If IsEmpty(toks) Then
        RckSealed()
    Else
        RckShapeOne(First(toks), Rest(toks))

Def RckShapeOne(t As String, more As List Of String) As RckShape
    Select Case t
        Case ":"
            RckShapeDef(more)
        Case "{", "["
            RckShapeSpan(t, RckCloseOf(t), more)
        Case "}", "]", ";"
            RckWrecked("unexpected " & t)
        Case Else
            RckShapeOf(more)

Rem A definition with no name after it is refused by RckDefine before the
Rem scanner is ever reached, so it can never be finished by another line.
Def RckShapeDef(more As List Of String) As RckShape
    If IsEmpty(more) Then
        RckWrecked("a definition needs a name after " & RckDefMark())
    Else
        RckShapeSpan(RckDefMark(), RckEndMark(), Rest(more))

Def RckShapeSpan(open As String, close As String, more As List Of String) As RckShape
    Select Case RckUpTo(more, open, close)
        Case RckSpanned(inner, tail)
            RckShapeIn(RckShapeOf(inner), tail)
        Case RckSpanBad(w)
            RckAjar(open)
        Case Else
            RckWrecked("the span did not scan")

Rem An inner construct left open inside one that has already closed is
Rem the one case that looks like waiting and is not.
Def RckShapeIn(inner As RckShape, tail As List Of String) As RckShape
    Select Case inner
        Case RckSealed
            RckShapeOf(tail)
        Case RckAjar(open)
            RckWrecked("unterminated " & open)
        Case Else
            inner

Rem The question a reader with more input actually asks, from the text
Rem rather than the tokens: should the next line be taken with this one?
Rem ONLY an ajar line answers yes. A line that will not tokenize does
Rem not, which is what keeps an unterminated string out of this: joining
Rem it would turn the line break inside it into a space, where a space is
Rem content and not separation.
Def RckWants(line As String) As Boolean
    Select Case RckTokens(line)
        Case Ok(toks)
            RckAjarly(RckShapeOf(toks))
        Case Else
            False

Def RckAjarly(found As RckShape) As Boolean
    Select Case found
        Case RckAjar(open)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Rem Physical lines joined the way the tokenizer reads them: one space.
Rem RckTokens separates on runs of whitespace and does not count them, so
Rem this is the same token stream the one-line form would have given.
Def RckJoinLines(buf As String, line As String) As String
    If buf = "" Then
        line
    Else
        Trim(buf & " " & line)

Rem ---- the evaluator ---------------------------------------------------
Rem One token at a time, threading a state and the output lines a shell
Rem will print. Everything that can go wrong comes back as RckBroke, and
Rem RckEval turns that into the state the line started from.

Def RckWalk(st As RckState, toks As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If IsEmpty(toks) Then
        RckWent(st, out)
    Else
        RckWalkOne(st, First(toks), Rest(toks), out)

Def RckWalkOne(st As RckState, t As String, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case t
        Case ":"
            RckDefine(st, more, out)
        Case "{"
            RckDoList(st, more, out)
        Case "["
            RckDoProg(st, more, out)
        Case "}", "]", ";"
            RckBroke("unexpected " & t, out)
        Case Else
            RckAtom(st, t, more, out)

Rem A token is a string, then a number, then a word — in that order, and
Rem "a number" is exactly what Val would accept, IsNumeric being the
Rem question Val answers. Val takes the whole token as one number or
Rem none at all, so 3X is a word here — an unknown one, refused by name —
Rem never 3. That strictness is the language's own and is inherited
Rem rather than corrected, so this tokenizer and the seeds can never
Rem disagree about what a number is.
Def RckAtom(st As RckState, t As String, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If RckIsText(t) Then
        RckPushedOn(st, t, CutStr(RckTextOf(t)), more, out)
    Else
        If IsNumeric(t) Then
            RckPushedOn(st, t, CutNum(Val(t)), more, out)
        Else
            RckNamed(st, Upper(t), more, out)

Def RckNamed(st As RckState, name As String, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckFind(st, name)
        Case Some(e)
            RckApply(st, e, more, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("unknown word " & name, out)

Rem HELP and VIEW read the word named NEXT rather than a string off the
Rem stack, because HELP MEAN is what someone types and "MEAN" HELP is
Rem what a language designer types. They are the only two words that
Rem look at the token stream, and they are still ordinary entries, so
Rem RckUnreg takes them away like anything else.
Def RckApply(st As RckState, e As RckEntry, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case WordBody(e)
        Case RckCore(tag)
            If tag = "HELP" Or tag = "VIEW" Then
                RckAsk(st, tag, more, out)
            Else
                RckWorked(st, e, more, out)
        Case Else
            RckWorked(st, e, more, out)

Def RckAsk(st As RckState, tag As String, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If IsEmpty(more) Then
        RckBroke(tag & " needs the name of a word after it", out)
    Else
        RckWalk(st, Rest(more), Concat(out, RckAsked(st, tag, Upper(First(more)))))

Def RckAsked(st As RckState, tag As String, name As String) As List Of String
    If tag = "VIEW" Then
        RckView(st, name)
    Else
        RckHelp(st, name)

Rem Every other word: take its arguments, run it, walk on. This is the one
Rem funnel every word application passes through, so it is where the
Rem forward history goes — except for the two words that own it.
Def RckWorked(st As RckState, e As RckEntry, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckArgsOf(RckMovedFor(st, e), e)
        Case RckHasArgs(st1, args)
            RckThen(RckRan(st1, e, args, out), more)
        Case RckArgsBad(w)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(WordName(e) & " could not take its arguments", out)

Def RckMovedFor(st As RckState, e As RckEntry) As RckState
    If RckOwnsFore(e) Then
        st
    Else
        RckMoved(st)

Def RckOwnsFore(e As RckEntry) As Boolean
    Select Case WordBody(e)
        Case RckCore(tag)
            tag = "UNDO" Or tag = "REDO"
        Case Else
            False

Def RckThen(r As RckRun, more As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case r
        Case RckWent(st2, out2)
            RckWalk(st2, more, out2)
        Case RckBroke(w, out2)
            RckBroke(w, out2)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("the word did not answer", { })

Def RckRan(st As RckState, e As RckEntry, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case WordBody(e)
        Case RckPure(f)
            RckDidWord(st, e, RckWorkDone(st, Call(f, args), out))
        Case RckTouch(f)
            RckDidWord(st, e, RckWorkDone(st, Call(f, args), out))
        Case RckPureOver(f)
            RckDidWord(st, e, RckWorkDone(st, Call(f, st, args), out))
        Case RckUser(ts, touch)
            RckRanUser(st, WordName(e), ts, args, out)
        Case RckCore(tag)
            RckDidWord(st, e, RckCoreDo(st, tag, args, out))
        Case Else
            RckBroke(WordName(e) & " has no body", out)

Rem The | row for a word that did not run a program. A user word and the
Rem combinators are left out because RckTraceBack has already reported
Rem them coming back, and a token gets one row.
Def RckDidWord(st As RckState, e As RckEntry, r As RckRun) As RckRun
    Select Case r
        Case RckWent(st2, out2)
            RckWentSaid(st, e, st2, out2)
        Case Else
            r

Def RckWentSaid(st As RckState, e As RckEntry, st2 As RckState, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If RckOwnsRow(e) Then
        RckWent(st2, out)
    Else
        RckWent(st2, RckTraceDid(st, WordName(e), st2, out))

Def RckOwnsRow(e As RckEntry) As Boolean
    Select Case WordBody(e)
        Case RckCore(tag)
            RckRunsProg(tag)
        Case Else
            False

Def RckWorkDone(st As RckState, w As RckWork, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case w
        Case RckVals(vs)
            RckWent(RckPushAll(st, vs), out)
        Case RckWorkBad(why)
            RckBroke(why, out)
        Case RckOpened(nm, kind, hand, data, shut)
            RckWorkBound(st, nm, kind, hand, data, shut, out)
        Case RckUpdated(nm, data, vs)
            RckWorkMoved(st, nm, data, vs, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("the word did not answer", out)

Rem Replacing a bound resource's data. The name has to be bound already:
Rem this is not a second way to open something, and a binding conjured
Rem here would have no closer.
Def RckWorkMoved(st As RckState, nm As String, data As Cell, vs As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If Not RckBoundTo(st, nm) Then
        RckBroke("there is nothing open called " & nm, out)
    Else
        RckWent(RckPushAll(RckRedata(st, nm, data), vs), out)

Rem The handle, the kind and the closer are kept exactly as they were —
Rem only the machine's own description of the resource moves on.
Def RckRedata(st As RckState, nm As String, data As Cell) As RckState
    With(st, RckOpen = Map(RckOpen(st), Fn(r) => RckRedataOne(r, nm, data)))

Def RckRedataOne(r As RckRes, nm As String, data As Cell) As RckRes
    If ResNameOf(r) = nm Then
        RckRes(ResNameOf(r), ResKindOf(r), ResHandOf(r), data, ResShut(r))
    Else
        r

Def RckWorkBound(st As RckState, nm As String, kind As String, hand As Number, data As Cell, shut As [ -- Boolean ], out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If RckBoundTo(st, nm) Then
        RckShutSpare(st, nm, shut, out)
    Else
        RckWent(RckBind(st, nm, kind, hand, data, shut), RckSaid(st, out, "ok: opened " & nm))

Rem The word opened the resource BEFORE it answered, so refusing the
Rem bind has to shut what it was handed. Leaving it open would leak the
Rem exact thing this table exists to keep hold of, and leak it on the
Rem error path, where nobody would look.
Def RckShutSpare(st As RckState, nm As String, shut As [ -- Boolean ], out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If Call(shut) Then
        RckBroke("there is already something open called " & nm, out)
    Else
        RckBroke("there is already something open called " & nm & ", and the one just opened did not shut cleanly", out)

Rem A user word: one cell in on a fresh stack, and whatever it leaves
Rem pushed back onto the caller's. The state it changed on the way —
Rem registers, dictionary, modes — comes back with it.
Def RckRanUser(st As RckState, name As String, ts As List Of String, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckSubIn(st, name, args, ts, out)
        Case RckWent(st2, out2)
            RckBackFrom(st, name, RckKeep(st, st2, Reverse(CutCells(RckStack(st2)))), out2)
        Case RckBroke(w, out2)
            RckBroke(w, out2)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(name & " did not answer", out)

Rem The nesting count goes up here and comes back down in RckKeep and
Rem RckSameBut, and the only thing it is read for is whether STO says
Rem "ok:". A register banked inside a word is an implementation detail of
Rem that word; banked from the entry line it is the answer to the line,
Rem which is what the worked sessions show.
Def RckSub(st As RckState, given As List Of Cell, toks As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    RckWalk(With(st, RckStack = CutOf(given), RckDeep = RckDeep(st) + 1), toks, out)

Rem The same, announced. Every sub-evaluation in the machine goes through
Rem here so that turning TRACE on cannot miss one: a user word's body, a
Rem { } literal, and every pass of every combinator.
Def RckSubIn(st As RckState, name As String, given As List Of Cell, toks As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    RckSub(st, given, toks, RckTraceInto(st, name, toks, out))

Rem What a program must leave: exactly one cell. A program that leaves
Rem none or two has been mistyped, and saying so beats quietly taking
Rem the top one and losing the rest.
Def RckOnly(word As String, st2 As RckState) As Result
    If CutDepth(RckStack(st2)) = 1 Then
        Ok(First(CutCells(RckStack(st2))))
    Else
        Err(word & " needs its program to leave one value, it left " & Str(CutDepth(RckStack(st2))), 0)

Rem ---- a program cell as a function of one number -----------------------
Rem
Rem WHAT THIS IS FOR. A machine whose word takes a quotation — eng's
Rem numeric derivative, its integrator, its root finders — wants a
Rem [ Number -- Number ] to Call. What the entry line supplies is a
Rem CutProg: a list of tokens, which is a thing to EVALUATE and not a
Rem thing to call. These two make one out of the other, and they are here
Rem rather than in a seed because nothing about them is a domain: they
Rem are the evaluator, pointed at one cell and asked for one number.
Rem
Rem TWO FORMS, AND THE PAIRING IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.
Rem
Rem   RckProgTry is the honest one and answers a Result. The program runs
Rem   on a fresh stack with x on it — the same sub-evaluation a
Rem   combinator's program gets — and is refused for the engine's own
Rem   reason if it will not evaluate, does not leave exactly one cell, or
Rem   leaves a cell that is not a NUMBER.
Rem
Rem   RckProgFn is the one a machine can actually accept, and it is TOTAL
Rem   because a [ Number -- Number ] has nowhere to put a refusal:
Rem   wherever RckProgTry would refuse, this answers 0. THAT IS A WRONG
Rem   NUMBER AND NOT A REFUSAL, which is why the two are published
Rem   together — the only safe way to hand RckProgFn to a machine is to
Rem   have asked RckProgTry first at every point the machine will
Rem   evaluate. A caller that knows its sample points ahead of time (a
Rem   Simpson rule, a sum over a range) can check all of them and be
Rem   exactly right; one that chooses them adaptively (a bisection, a
Rem   golden section) can check the bracket it will stay inside, which is
Rem   where the guarantee stops. Neither can abort the mill either way:
Rem   the worst RckProgFn does is answer 0.
Rem
Rem WHAT THE PROGRAM CAN SEE is the caller's whole session — the
Rem dictionary including anything the user has defined, the registers,
Rem the angle mode. That is why the state is a parameter and why a word
Rem taking a program is RckPureOver: [ SQ ] with SQ defined a line
Rem earlier has to work, and "STO before, RCL inside" is the documented
Rem way a program reaches a value that is not its element.
Rem
Rem WHAT THE PROGRAM CHANGES IS DISCARDED. A sub-evaluation normally
Rem threads its state home, but a function a solver calls two hundred
Rem times cannot leave two hundred dictionaries behind, and a derivative
Rem that quietly redefined a word would be a derivative nobody could
Rem read. So this is the one sub-evaluation whose state does not come
Rem back: it answers a number and nothing else.

Def RckProgFn(st As RckState, ts As List Of String) As [ Number -- Number ]
    Fn(x) => RckProgOr0(st, ts, x)

Def RckProgOr0(st As RckState, ts As List Of String, x As Number) As Number
    Select Case RckProgTry(RckProgOpen(), st, ts, x)
        Case Ok(y)
            y
        Case Else
            0

Rem THE GENERAL FORM IS RckProgOn: one CELL in, one CELL out, which is
Rem what a program actually is — the number pair below is a special case
Rem of it and is written that way rather than beside it, so the two
Rem cannot come to disagree about what "the program did not answer"
Rem means. A word that applies a program to any cell (a bare CALL) wants
Rem this one; the numeric machines want RckProgTry.
Def RckProgOn(word As String, st As RckState, ts As List Of String, c As Cell) As Result
    RckProgRan(word, RckSub(st, { c }, ts, { }))

Def RckProgRan(word As String, r As RckRun) As Result
    Select Case r
        Case RckWent(st2, out)
            RckOnly(word, st2)
        Case RckBroke(w, out)
            Err(w, 0)
        Case Else
            Err(word & ": the program did not answer", 0)

Def RckProgTry(word As String, st As RckState, ts As List Of String, x As Number) As Result
    RckProgNumOf(word, RckProgOn(word, st, ts, CutNum(x)))

Def RckProgNumOf(word As String, r As Result) As Result
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(c)
            RckProgNum(word, c)
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & ": the program did not answer", 0)

Def RckProgNum(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutNum(y)
            Ok(y)
        Case Else
            Err(RckKindWhy(word, "a program leaving one NUMBER", c), 0)

Rem ---- the two literal forms -------------------------------------------

Rem { ... } evaluates its tokens on a fresh sub-stack and pushes whatever
Rem remains as ONE list. There is no second grammar inside the braces:
Rem { 1 2 3 } is three cells collected, { 1 2 + } is { 3 }, and nesting
Rem falls out of the rule rather than being another case in it.
Def RckDoList(st As RckState, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckUpTo(more, RckListOpen(), RckListClose())
        Case RckSpanned(inner, tail)
            RckThen(RckListMade(st, inner, out), tail)
        Case RckSpanBad(w)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("the list did not scan", out)

Def RckListMade(st As RckState, inner As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckSubIn(st, RckListOpen(), { }, inner, out)
        Case RckWent(st2, out2)
            RckBackFrom(st, RckListOpen(), RckKeep(st, st2, { CutList(Reverse(CutCells(RckStack(st2)))) }), out2)
        Case RckBroke(w, out2)
            RckBroke(w, out2)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("the list did not answer", out)

Rem [ ... ] is not evaluated: it becomes a program cell holding the
Rem tokens as typed, which is what makes it printable, saveable and
Rem re-readable, and what keeps closures out of the language entirely.
Def RckDoProg(st As RckState, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckUpTo(more, RckProgOpen(), RckProgClose())
        Case RckSpanned(inner, tail)
            RckPushedOn(st, RckProgOpen(), CutProg(inner), tail, out)
        Case RckSpanBad(w)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("the program did not scan", out)

Rem ---- defining a word -------------------------------------------------

Def RckDefine(st As RckState, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If IsEmpty(more) Then
        RckBroke("a definition needs a name after " & RckDefMark(), out)
    Else
        RckDefineOn(st, First(more), Rest(more), out)

Def RckDefineOn(st As RckState, name As String, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckUpTo(more, RckDefMark(), RckEndMark())
        Case RckSpanned(body, tail)
            RckThen(RckDefined(st, name, body, out), tail)
        Case RckSpanBad(w)
            RckBroke("a definition ends with " & RckEndMark() & ", and this one does not", out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("the definition did not scan", out)

Def RckDefined(st As RckState, name As String, body As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckDef(st, name, body)
        Case RckNext(st2, out2)
            RckWent(st2, Concat(out, out2))
        Case RckStop(st2, w, out2)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("the definition did not answer", out)

Rem VALIDATED WHERE IT IS WRITTEN, not where it is first run. : BAD FROB ;
Rem is refused now, naming FROB, because a dictionary that accepts a
Rem definition and fails on it a week later is worse than one that
Rem refuses it while the person who typed it is still looking.
Def RckDef(st As RckState, name As String, toks As List Of String) As RckLine
    RckDefWhen(st, Upper(name), toks, RckVetName(Upper(name)))

Def RckDefWhen(st As RckState, name As String, toks As List Of String, bad As String) As RckLine
    If bad <> "" Then
        RckStop(st, bad, { })
    Else
        RckDefVetted(st, name, toks, RckVet(st, name, toks))

Def RckDefVetted(st As RckState, name As String, toks As List Of String, why As String) As RckLine
    If why <> "" Then
        RckStop(st, why, { })
    Else
        RckNext(RckPut(st, RckUserEntry(st, name, toks)), { "ok: " & name & " defined" })

Rem A user word's help is its own token listing, and its arity is one:
Rem there is nothing to declare because there is nothing to choose.
Def RckUserEntry(st As RckState, name As String, toks As List Of String) As RckEntry
    RckEntry(name, 1, RckUser(toks, RckTouchy(st, toks)), "( x -- ... )", RckDefMark() & " " & name & " " & Join(toks, " ") & " " & RckEndMark(), RckUserName())

Def RckTouchy(st As RckState, toks As List Of String) As Boolean
    Any(toks, Fn(t) => RckTouchesName(st, t))

Def RckTouchesName(st As RckState, t As String) As Boolean
    Select Case RckFind(st, Upper(t))
        Case Some(e)
            RckTouches(e)
        Case Else
            False

Def RckVetName(name As String) As String
    If name = "" Then
        "a definition needs a name"
    Else
        If RckIsMark(name) Or RckIsText(name) Or IsNumeric(name) Then
            "a word cannot be called " & name
        Else
            ""

Def RckIsMark(t As String) As Boolean
    Contains({ ":", ";", "{", "}", "[", "]" }, t)

Rem The body, word by word. Everything it names must already exist, and
Rem the name being defined must not: an acyclic call graph is what makes
Rem "nothing keyable aborts" true of : LOOP LOOP ; as well as of 1 0 /.
Def RckVet(st As RckState, name As String, toks As List Of String) As String
    If IsEmpty(toks) Then
        ""
    Else
        RckVetOne(st, name, First(toks), Rest(toks))

Def RckVetOne(st As RckState, name As String, t As String, more As List Of String) As String
    Select Case t
        Case ":", ";"
            "a definition cannot hold another definition"
        Case "{", "["
            RckVetSpan(st, name, t, more)
        Case "}", "]"
            "unexpected " & t & " in the body of " & name
        Case Else
            RckVetWord(st, name, t, more)

Def RckVetSpan(st As RckState, name As String, open As String, more As List Of String) As String
    Select Case RckUpTo(more, open, RckCloseOf(open))
        Case RckSpanned(inner, tail)
            RckVetBoth(st, name, inner, tail)
        Case RckSpanBad(w)
            w & " in the body of " & name
        Case Else
            "the body of " & name & " did not scan"

Def RckVetBoth(st As RckState, name As String, inner As List Of String, tail As List Of String) As String
    If RckVet(st, name, inner) <> "" Then
        RckVet(st, name, inner)
    Else
        RckVet(st, name, tail)

Def RckVetWord(st As RckState, name As String, t As String, more As List Of String) As String
    If RckIsText(t) Or IsNumeric(t) Then
        RckVet(st, name, more)
    Else
        If Upper(t) = name Then
            "a word cannot be defined in terms of itself: " & name
        Else
            If RckKnows(st, Upper(t)) Then
                RckVet(st, name, more)
            Else
                "unknown word " & Upper(t)

Rem ---- the core words that reach the state -----------------------------

Def RckCoreDo(st As RckState, tag As String, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case tag
        Case "DUP"
            RckFromOut(st, CutDup(RckStack(st)), out)
        Case "DROP"
            RckFromOut(st, CutDrop(RckStack(st)), out)
        Case "SWAP"
            RckFromOut(st, CutSwap(RckStack(st)), out)
        Case "OVER"
            RckFromOut(st, CutOver(RckStack(st)), out)
        Case "NIP"
            RckFromOut(st, CutNip(RckStack(st)), out)
        Case "TUCK"
            RckFromOut(st, CutTuck(RckStack(st)), out)
        Case "ROT"
            RckFromOut(st, CutRot(RckStack(st)), out)
        Case "ROLL"
            RckLevel(st, "ROLL", args, out)
        Case "PICK"
            RckLevel(st, "PICK", args, out)
        Case "DEPTH"
            RckWent(RckPushed(st, CutNum(CutDepth(RckStack(st)))), out)
        Case "CLEAR"
            RckWent(With(st, RckStack = CutClear(RckStack(st))), out)
        Case "GATHER"
            RckGather(st, args, out)
        Case "UNPACK"
            RckUnpack(st, args, out)
        Case "LASTX"
            RckLastOf(st, out)
        Case "STO"
            RckSto(st, args, out)
        Case "RCL"
            RckRcl(st, args, out)
        Case "CLOSE"
            RckClose(st, args, out)
        Case "CLOSEALL"
            RckCloseAll(st, out)
        Case "BOUND"
            RckWent(RckPushed(st, CutList(Map(RckOpen(st), RckResCell))), out)
        Case "UNDO"
            RckUndo(st, out)
        Case "REDO"
            RckRedo(st, out)
        Case "TRACE"
            RckWent(With(st, RckTrace = True), RckSaid(st, out, "trace on"))
        Case "NOTRACE"
            RckWent(With(st, RckTrace = False), RckSaid(st, out, "trace off"))
        Case "TAPE"
            RckWent(RckPushed(st, CutNum(Length(RckTape(st)))), out)
        Case "CLEARTAPE"
            RckWent(With(st, RckTape = { }), out)
        Case "DEG"
            RckWent(With(st, RckAngle = RckDegrees()), out)
        Case "RAD"
            RckWent(With(st, RckAngle = RckRadians()), out)
        Case "STD"
            RckWent(With(st, RckFmt = CutFmtStd()), out)
        Case "WIDTH"
            RckWidthSet(st, args, out)
        Case "WIDTH?"
            RckWent(RckPushed(st, CutNum(RckWidth(st))), out)
        Case "FIX"
            RckDigits(st, "FIX", CutModeFix(), args, out)
        Case "SCI"
            RckDigits(st, "SCI", CutModeSci(), args, out)
        Case "ENG"
            RckDigits(st, "ENG", CutModeEng(), args, out)
        Case "SIN"
            RckTrigIn(st, "SIN", args, RckAlways, "", Sin, out)
        Case "COS"
            RckTrigIn(st, "COS", args, RckAlways, "", Cos, out)
        Case "TAN"
            RckTrigIn(st, "TAN", args, RckAlways, "", Tan, out)
        Case "ASIN"
            RckTrigOut(st, "ASIN", args, RckUnit, "has no answer outside -1 to 1", Asin, out)
        Case "ACOS"
            RckTrigOut(st, "ACOS", args, RckUnit, "has no answer outside -1 to 1", Acos, out)
        Case "ATAN"
            RckTrigOut(st, "ATAN", args, RckAlways, "", Atn, out)
        Case "ATAN2"
            RckAtan2(st, args, out)
        Case "IFT"
            RckIft(st, args, out)
        Case "IFTE"
            RckIfte(st, args, out)
        Case "TIMES"
            RckTimes(st, args, out)
        Case "MAP"
            RckMap(st, args, out)
        Case "FILTER"
            RckFilter(st, args, out)
        Case "FOLD"
            RckFold(st, args, out)
        Case "WORDS"
            RckWent(st, Concat(out, RckWords(st)))
        Case Else
            RckBroke("the engine has no word tagged " & tag, out)

Rem cuttle's algebra already answers; this turns its answer into the
Rem evaluator's. The refused stack it hands back is discarded because
Rem the line, not the word, is the transaction.
Def RckFromOut(st As RckState, o As CutOut, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case o
        Case CutDone(s2)
            RckWent(With(st, RckStack = s2), out)
        Case CutOutBad(w, s2)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("the stack did not answer", out)

Def RckLevel(st As RckState, word As String, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckNumArg(word, First(args))
        Case Ok(n)
            RckFromOut(st, RckLevelAt(st, word, n), out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", out)

Def RckLevelAt(st As RckState, word As String, n As Number) As CutOut
    If word = "ROLL" Then
        CutRoll(RckStack(st), n)
    Else
        CutPick(RckStack(st), n)

Rem GATHER and UNPACK are the two explicit crossings between a run of
Rem cells and one aggregate cell. Everywhere else an aggregate occupies
Rem exactly one level, however many elements it holds.
Def RckGather(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckWholeArg("GATHER", First(args))
        Case Ok(n)
            RckGatherN(st, n, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("GATHER did not answer", out)

Def RckGatherN(st As RckState, n As Number, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If CutDepth(RckStack(st)) < n Then
        RckBroke(CutShort("GATHER", n, CutDepth(RckStack(st))), out)
    Else
        RckWent(RckPushed(With(st, RckStack = Stack(DropN(CutCells(RckStack(st)), n))), CutList(Reverse(Taken(CutCells(RckStack(st)), n)))), out)

Def RckUnpack(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckListArg("UNPACK", First(args))
        Case Ok(xs)
            RckWent(RckPushAll(st, Append(xs, CutNum(Length(xs)))), out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("UNPACK did not answer", out)

Def RckLastOf(st As RckState, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckLastX(st)
        Case Some(c)
            RckWent(RckPushed(st, c), out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("LASTX has nothing yet: no word has taken an argument", out)

Rem Registers are keyed by the string as typed. They hold data rather
Rem than names, so they are NOT folded to upper the way dictionary words
Rem are, and "n" and "N" are two registers.
Def RckSto(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckTextArg("STO", Nth(args, 2))
        Case Ok(k)
            RckWent(With(st, RckRegs = DictPut(RckRegs(st), k, Nth(args, 1))), RckSaid(st, out, "ok: " & k))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("STO did not answer", out)

Def RckRcl(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckTextArg("RCL", First(args))
        Case Ok(k)
            RckRclAt(st, k, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("RCL did not answer", out)

Def RckSaid(st As RckState, out As List Of String, line As String) As List Of String
    If RckDeep(st) = 0 Then
        Append(out, line)
    Else
        out

Def RckRclAt(st As RckState, k As String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If DictHas(RckRegs(st), k) Then
        RckWent(RckPushed(st, DictGet(RckRegs(st), k)), out)
    Else
        RckBroke("there is nothing in register " & k, out)

Rem ---- CLOSE, CLOSEALL, BOUND ------------------------------------------

Rem A name/kind pair, which is all a session is shown of a resource.
Rem The handle is deliberately not in here: putting it in a cell would
Rem be putting it on the stack, which is the thing R3.5(b) forbids and
Rem the reason this table holds names instead.
Def RckResCell(r As RckRes) As Cell
    CutPair(Pair(CutStr(ResNameOf(r)), CutStr(ResKindOf(r))))

Def RckClose(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckTextArg("CLOSE", First(args))
        Case Ok(k)
            RckCloseAt(st, k, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("CLOSE did not answer", out)

Def RckCloseAt(st As RckState, k As String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If Not RckBoundTo(st, k) Then
        RckBroke("there is nothing open called " & k, out)
    Else
        RckClosed(st, k, First(RckResOf(st, k)), out)

Rem THE NAME GOES WHATEVER THE CLOSER ANSWERS. A closer that reports
Rem trouble has still had its one chance, and a name left bound to
Rem something nobody can shut is worse than a name let go — the session
Rem could neither retry it nor open another under it. So the trouble is
Rem SAID and the name is released either way.
Def RckClosed(st As RckState, k As String, r As RckRes, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If RckShutting(r) Then
        RckWent(RckUnbind(st, k), RckSaid(st, out, "ok: closed " & k))
    Else
        RckWent(RckUnbind(st, k), RckSaid(st, out, "ok: let go of " & k & ", which did not shut cleanly"))

Rem Answers how many there WERE, which is the useful number: after this
Rem there are none, and BOUND would answer the empty list either way.
Def RckCloseAll(st As RckState, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    RckWent(RckPushed(RckShutAll(st), CutNum(Length(RckOpen(st)))), out)

Def RckShutAll(st As RckState) As RckState
    Fold(RckOpen(st), st, RckShutInto)

Def RckShutInto(st As RckState, r As RckRes) As RckState
    RckUnbind(RckKept(st, RckShutting(r)), ResNameOf(r))

Rem The state, unchanged, once the closer has been called for its
Rem effect. CLOSEALL reports a count rather than a per-resource verdict,
Rem so the answer is taken and dropped here on purpose.
Def RckKept(st As RckState, shut As Boolean) As RckState
    If shut Then
        st
    Else
        st

Rem The history holds the stack each line STARTED from, newest first, and
Rem the current line's entry is already on it by the time UNDO runs — so
Rem going back one transaction means stepping over that entry to the one
Rem beneath. A session one line old has nothing to go back to and says so.
Def RckUndo(st As RckState, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If Length(RckHist(st)) < 2 Then
        RckBroke("there is nothing to undo", out)
    Else
        RckWent(RckUndone(st), out)

Rem Stepping back BANKS the stack it is stepping away from, because this
Rem is where it would otherwise be lost: the two history entries go, and
Rem nothing else in the state remembers what was on screen a moment ago.
Def RckUndone(st As RckState) As RckState
    With(With(With(st, RckFore = Taken(Prepend(RckStack(st), RckFore(st)), RckHistCap())), RckStack = Nth(RckHist(st), 2)), RckHist = DropN(RckHist(st), 2))

Rem REDO takes one back off. It does NOT push to the history, because the
Rem line REDO arrived on already did that through RckRemember — which is
Rem what makes the two exact inverses however many times they alternate.
Def RckRedo(st As RckState, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If IsEmpty(RckFore(st)) Then
        RckBroke("there is nothing to redo", out)
    Else
        RckWent(RckRedone(st), out)

Def RckRedone(st As RckState) As RckState
    With(With(st, RckStack = First(RckFore(st))), RckFore = Rest(RckFore(st)))

Def RckRemember(st As RckState) As RckState
    With(st, RckHist = Taken(Prepend(RckStack(st), RckHist(st)), RckHistCap()))

Def RckDigits(st As RckState, word As String, mode As String, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckWholeArg(word, First(args))
        Case Ok(n)
            RckDigitsAt(st, word, mode, n, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", out)

Def RckDigitsAt(st As RckState, word As String, mode As String, n As Number, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If n > 11 Then
        RckBroke(word & " shows at most 11 places, not " & Str(n), out)
    Else
        RckWent(With(st, RckFmt = CutFmt(mode, n)), out)

Rem ---- the word width --------------------------------------------------
Rem VALIDATED HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. Every word that later reads
Rem RckWidth reads a number already known to be whole and inside 1..53,
Rem so no seed repeats this check and no seed can forget it. RckWholeArg
Rem has already refused a negative and a fraction by the time this runs,
Rem which leaves only the two ends to name.
Rem
Rem It sets a mode and pushes nothing, exactly as FIX and DEG do. WIDTH?
Rem is the one that answers, and it is a separate word because a name
Rem cannot carry two arities — the price this design pays, paid in a
Rem name rather than in a second mode.

Def RckWidthSet(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckWholeArg("WIDTH", First(args))
        Case Ok(n)
            RckWidthAt(st, n, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("WIDTH did not answer", out)

Def RckWidthAt(st As RckState, n As Number, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If n < 1 Or n > RckMaxWidth() Then
        RckBroke("WIDTH needs a width from 1 to " & Str(RckMaxWidth()) & ", got " & Str(n), out)
    Else
        RckWent(With(st, RckWidth = n), out)

Rem ---- trigonometry, and the one mode there is -------------------------
Rem The angle mode governs evaluation INSIDE the engine and travels no
Rem further: a number leaving through RckShow carries no unit, because a
Rem degree, a radian and a step length are all one numeric kind. It
Rem exists for entry and only for entry — 30 SIN being 0.5 is the whole
Rem reason a handheld has a DEG key. The tree's own answer to degrees
Rem stays conversion, which is why no machine gains a mode from this and
Rem why seed-math must not bridge Rad and Deg.

Def RckInAngle(st As RckState, x As Number) As Number
    If RckAngle(st) = RckDegrees() Then
        x * Pi() / 180
    Else
        x

Def RckOutAngle(st As RckState, x As Number) As Number
    If RckAngle(st) = RckDegrees() Then
        x * 180 / Pi()
    Else
        x

Def RckTrigIn(st As RckState, word As String, args As List Of Cell, fits As [ Number -- Boolean ], why As String, f As [ Number -- Number ], out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckNumArg(word, First(args))
        Case Ok(x)
            RckTrigDone(st, word, x, RckInAngle(st, x), fits, why, f, False, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", out)

Def RckTrigOut(st As RckState, word As String, args As List Of Cell, fits As [ Number -- Boolean ], why As String, f As [ Number -- Number ], out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckNumArg(word, First(args))
        Case Ok(x)
            RckTrigDone(st, word, x, x, fits, why, f, True, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", out)

Def RckTrigDone(st As RckState, word As String, raw As Number, arg As Number, fits As [ Number -- Boolean ], why As String, f As [ Number -- Number ], back As Boolean, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If Not Call(fits, raw) Then
        RckBroke(word & " " & why, out)
    Else
        RckWent(RckPushed(st, CutNum(RckTrigVal(st, arg, f, back))), out)

Def RckTrigVal(st As RckState, arg As Number, f As [ Number -- Number ], back As Boolean) As Number
    If back Then
        RckOutAngle(st, Call(f, arg))
    Else
        Call(f, arg)

Def RckAtan2(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckNumArg("ATAN2", Nth(args, 1))
        Case Ok(y)
            RckAtan2On(st, y, Nth(args, 2), out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("ATAN2 did not answer", out)

Def RckAtan2On(st As RckState, y As Number, c As Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckNumArg("ATAN2", c)
        Case Ok(x)
            RckWent(RckPushed(st, CutNum(RckOutAngle(st, Atn2(y, x)))), out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("ATAN2 did not answer", out)

Rem ---- the combinators -------------------------------------------------
Rem They take quoted programs off the stack and run them through this
Rem same evaluator, which is what keeps closures out of the language: a
Rem bridged word that wanted a function is not bridged, because a token
Rem list and a combinator cover the case instead.
Rem
Rem Every one of them seeds a fresh stack with the program's ELEMENT and
Rem requires exactly one cell back. Anything else the program needs comes
Rem out of a register, which is the whole of R4.17's rationale and the
Rem reason a MAP program can call a user word that itself filters.

Def RckIft(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckBoolArg("IFT", Nth(args, 2))
        Case Ok(flag)
            RckIftOn(st, flag, Nth(args, 1), Nth(args, 3), out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("IFT did not answer", out)

Def RckIftOn(st As RckState, flag As Boolean, x As Cell, prog As Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    If flag Then
        RckOneShot(st, "IFT", x, prog, out)
    Else
        RckWent(RckPushed(st, x), out)

Def RckIfte(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckBoolArg("IFTE", Nth(args, 2))
        Case Ok(flag)
            RckOneShot(st, "IFTE", Nth(args, 1), Ifte(flag, Nth(args, 3), Nth(args, 4)), out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("IFTE did not answer", out)

Rem One program, one cell in, one cell out — the shape IFT, IFTE and
Rem every pass of TIMES, MAP, FILTER and FOLD are made of.
Def RckOneShot(st As RckState, word As String, x As Cell, prog As Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckProgArg(word, prog)
        Case Ok(ts)
            RckShotAt(st, word, { x }, ts, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", out)

Def RckShotAt(st As RckState, word As String, given As List Of Cell, ts As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckSubIn(st, word, given, ts, out)
        Case RckWent(st2, out2)
            RckShotDone(st, word, st2, out2)
        Case RckBroke(w, out2)
            RckBroke(w, out2)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", out)

Def RckShotDone(st As RckState, word As String, st2 As RckState, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckOnly(word, st2)
        Case Ok(v)
            RckBackFrom(st, word, RckKeep(st, st2, { v }), out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", out)

Rem TIMES, MAP, FILTER and FOLD all fold a flow: the break travels in the
Rem accumulator rather than in the call depth, so a six-hundred element
Rem list costs six hundred iterations of a builtin Fold and no Shoddy
Rem stack at all.

Def RckFlowOf(st As RckState, vals As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckFlow
    RckFlowing(st, vals, out)

Def RckMap(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    RckOverList(st, "MAP", args, out, False)

Def RckFilter(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    RckOverList(st, "FILTER", args, out, True)

Def RckOverList(st As RckState, word As String, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String, keep As Boolean) As RckRun
    Select Case RckListArg(word, Nth(args, 1))
        Case Ok(xs)
            RckOverProg(st, word, xs, Nth(args, 2), out, keep)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", out)

Def RckOverProg(st As RckState, word As String, xs As List Of Cell, prog As Cell, out As List Of String, keep As Boolean) As RckRun
    Select Case RckProgArg(word, prog)
        Case Ok(ts)
            RckListed(st, word, Fold(xs, RckFlowOf(st, { }, out), Fn(a, x) => RckOverStep(a, x, word, ts, keep)))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", out)

Def RckOverStep(a As RckFlow, x As Cell, word As String, ts As List Of String, keep As Boolean) As RckFlow
    Select Case a
        Case RckFlowing(st, done, out)
            RckOverRan(st, done, x, word, keep, RckSubIn(st, word, { x }, ts, out))
        Case Else
            a

Def RckOverRan(st As RckState, done As List Of Cell, x As Cell, word As String, keep As Boolean, r As RckRun) As RckFlow
    Select Case r
        Case RckWent(st2, out2)
            RckOverOne(st, done, x, word, keep, st2, out2)
        Case RckBroke(w, out2)
            RckFlowBad(w, out2)
        Case Else
            RckFlowBad(word & " did not answer", { })

Def RckOverOne(st As RckState, done As List Of Cell, x As Cell, word As String, keep As Boolean, st2 As RckState, out As List Of String) As RckFlow
    Select Case RckOnly(word, st2)
        Case Ok(v)
            RckOverKept(RckSameBut(st, st2), done, x, word, keep, v, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckFlowBad(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckFlowBad(word & " did not answer", out)

Rem MAP collects what the program answered; FILTER collects the element
Rem the program answered True for, and insists the answer was a boolean.
Def RckOverKept(st As RckState, done As List Of Cell, x As Cell, word As String, keep As Boolean, v As Cell, out As List Of String) As RckFlow
    If Not keep Then
        RckFlowing(st, Prepend(v, done), out)
    Else
        RckOverTest(st, done, x, v, out)

Def RckOverTest(st As RckState, done As List Of Cell, x As Cell, v As Cell, out As List Of String) As RckFlow
    Select Case RckBoolArg("FILTER", v)
        Case Ok(p)
            RckFlowing(st, Ifte(p, Prepend(x, done), done), out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckFlowBad(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckFlowBad("FILTER did not answer", out)

Rem The collected cells come back reversed, since each pass prepended.
Def RckListed(st As RckState, word As String, a As RckFlow) As RckRun
    Select Case a
        Case RckFlowing(st2, done, out)
            RckBackFrom(st, word, RckKeep(st, st2, { CutList(Reverse(done)) }), out)
        Case RckFlowBad(w, out)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", { })

Def RckFold(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckListArg("FOLD", Nth(args, 1))
        Case Ok(xs)
            RckFoldProg(st, xs, Nth(args, 2), Nth(args, 3), out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("FOLD did not answer", out)

Def RckFoldProg(st As RckState, xs As List Of Cell, init As Cell, prog As Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckProgArg("FOLD", prog)
        Case Ok(ts)
            RckOnlyOut(st, "FOLD", Fold(xs, RckFlowOf(st, { init }, out), Fn(a, x) => RckFoldStep(a, x, ts)))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("FOLD did not answer", out)

Def RckFoldStep(a As RckFlow, x As Cell, ts As List Of String) As RckFlow
    Select Case a
        Case RckFlowing(st, acc, out)
            RckPassed(st, "FOLD", RckSubIn(st, "FOLD", { First(acc), x }, ts, out))
        Case Else
            a

Def RckTimes(st As RckState, args As List Of Cell, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckWholeArg("TIMES", Nth(args, 3))
        Case Ok(n)
            RckTimesProg(st, Nth(args, 1), Nth(args, 2), n, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("TIMES did not answer", out)

Def RckTimesProg(st As RckState, x As Cell, prog As Cell, n As Number, out As List Of String) As RckRun
    Select Case RckProgArg("TIMES", prog)
        Case Ok(ts)
            RckOnlyOut(st, "TIMES", Fold(Range(1, n), RckFlowOf(st, { x }, out), Fn(a, k) => RckTimesStep(a, ts)))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke("TIMES did not answer", out)

Def RckTimesStep(a As RckFlow, ts As List Of String) As RckFlow
    Select Case a
        Case RckFlowing(st, acc, out)
            RckPassed(st, "TIMES", RckSubIn(st, "TIMES", { First(acc) }, ts, out))
        Case Else
            a

Rem One pass of FOLD or TIMES: the running answer becomes the flow's one
Rem carried cell, and the state it changed threads on.
Def RckPassed(st As RckState, word As String, r As RckRun) As RckFlow
    Select Case r
        Case RckWent(st2, out2)
            RckPassedOne(st, word, st2, out2)
        Case RckBroke(w, out2)
            RckFlowBad(w, out2)
        Case Else
            RckFlowBad(word & " did not answer", { })

Def RckPassedOne(st As RckState, word As String, st2 As RckState, out As List Of String) As RckFlow
    Select Case RckOnly(word, st2)
        Case Ok(v)
            RckFlowing(RckSameBut(st, st2), { v }, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckFlowBad(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckFlowBad(word & " did not answer", out)

Def RckOnlyOut(st As RckState, word As String, a As RckFlow) As RckRun
    Select Case a
        Case RckFlowing(st2, acc, out)
            RckBackFrom(st, word, RckKeep(st, st2, { First(acc) }), out)
        Case RckFlowBad(w, out)
            RckBroke(w, out)
        Case Else
            RckBroke(word & " did not answer", { })

Rem ---- the line --------------------------------------------------------
Rem Everything above works on tokens; this is the only door a shell needs.

Def RckEval(st As RckState, line As String) As RckLine
    Select Case RckTokens(line)
        Case Ok(toks)
            RckLineDone(st, line, RckWalk(RckRemember(st), toks, { }))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckStop(RckTapedBad(st, line, { }, w), w, { })
        Case Else
            RckStop(RckTapedBad(st, line, { }, "the line did not read"), "the line did not read", { })

Rem The error case hands back ST — the state as the line found it, with
Rem no history entry and nothing the line managed to do before it failed.
Rem The TAPE is the one thing it hands back changed, because a refusal is
Rem something the user saw and a tape that omitted it would be a record of
Rem a session that did not happen.
Def RckLineDone(st As RckState, line As String, r As RckRun) As RckLine
    Select Case r
        Case RckWent(st2, out)
            RckNext(RckTapedOk(st, st2, line, out), out)
        Case RckBroke(w, out)
            RckStop(RckTapedBad(st, line, out, w), w, out)
        Case Else
            RckStop(RckTapedBad(st, line, { }, "the line did not answer"), "the line did not answer", { })

Rem ---- the tape --------------------------------------------------------
Rem What the shell showed, kept in the state: the entry line, whatever the
Rem line said, and the stack AS RENDERED. RckShow and not RckRows, because
Rem the tape and the screen have to be the same text — that is the whole
Rem reason [ empty ] and [ depth n ] belong to the engine's render.
Rem
Rem RckLoad does not come through here, so loading a file of definitions
Rem leaves no rows: the tape is a record of a session, not of a startup.

Def RckTapedOk(st0 As RckState, st2 As RckState, line As String, out As List Of String) As RckState
    If RckTapeCleared(st0, st2) Then
        st2
    Else
        RckTapeAdd(st2, Concat(Prepend(RckTapeMark() & line, out), RckShow(st2)))

Rem A line that deliberately made the tape SHORTER is not itself recorded.
Rem CLEARTAPE tears the paper off, and paper torn off does not come back
Rem with the tearing written on it — without this, the one word whose job
Rem is to empty the tape could never leave it empty.
Def RckTapeCleared(st0 As RckState, st2 As RckState) As Boolean
    Length(RckTape(st2)) < Length(RckTape(st0))

Def RckTapedBad(st As RckState, line As String, out As List Of String, why As String) As RckState
    RckTapeAdd(st, Concat(Append(Prepend(RckTapeMark() & line, out), RckWhyMark() & why), RckShow(st)))

Def RckTapeMark() As String
    "> "

Def RckWhyMark() As String
    "?: "

Def RckTapeAdd(st As RckState, rows As List Of String) As RckState
    With(st, RckTape = RckTapeKept(Concat(RckTape(st), rows)))

Rem Bounded from the OLD end. The tape reads oldest first, so keeping the
Rem last RckTapeCap rows means dropping from the front — and a single line
Rem long enough to overrun the cap on its own still leaves the cap's worth
Rem of itself rather than nothing.
Def RckTapeKept(rows As List Of String) As List Of String
    If Length(rows) <= RckTapeCap() Then
        rows
    Else
        DropN(rows, Length(rows) - RckTapeCap())

Rem ---- the user dictionary as text -------------------------------------
Rem Split deliberately: the engine makes the lines and reads them back,
Rem and THE SHELL OWNS THE PATH. A config file beside the executable is
Rem one host's convention; a hosted mill writes to a sandboxed app-data
Rem path this machine could not know. Split this way, one engine serves
Rem both.

Def RckSave(st As RckState) As List Of String
    Map(Filter(RckDict(st), RckIsUser), RckDefLine)

Rem Load is evaluation, so a saved line is validated exactly as a typed
Rem one is — and a malformed line refuses the WHOLE load, leaving the
Rem dictionary as it was rather than half-populated.
Rem
Rem EACH LINE REMEMBERS, the same as a typed one, through RckRemember —
Rem without it a loaded file's own lines never grew RckHist, so a script
Rem that LOADs and then tries UNDO found nothing to undo even though
Rem typing the identical lines at a prompt works. RckHist is not the
Rem tape: RckLoad still writes no tape rows of its own (below), only
Rem history for UNDO/REDO to step through.
Def RckLoad(st As RckState, lines As List Of String) As RckLine
    RckLoadOn(st, st, "", lines, { })

Rem A file may write a definition, a list or a program over several lines
Rem exactly as they may be typed over several, so the loader carries what
Rem it has read and not yet closed. A FILE THAT ENDS MID-LINE REFUSES,
Rem where a session that ends mid-line simply closes: a file is a finished
Rem thing with a mistake in it, and a half-read definition is the mistake.
Def RckLoadOn(orig As RckState, st As RckState, buf As String, lines As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckLine
    If IsEmpty(lines) Then
        RckLoadEnd(orig, st, buf, out)
    Else
        RckLoadStep(orig, st, RckJoinLines(buf, First(lines)), Rest(lines), out)

Def RckLoadStep(orig As RckState, st As RckState, joined As String, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckLine
    If RckWants(joined) Then
        RckLoadOn(orig, st, joined, more, out)
    Else
        RckLoadLine(orig, st, joined, more, out)

Def RckLoadEnd(orig As RckState, st As RckState, buf As String, out As List Of String) As RckLine
    If buf = "" Then
        RckNext(st, out)
    Else
        RckStop(orig, RckLoadShort(), Concat(out, RckLoadFailRow(st, buf, { }, RckLoadShort())))

Def RckLoadShort() As String
    "the file ends before this line closes"

Def RckLoadLine(orig As RckState, st As RckState, line As String, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckLine
    Select Case RckTokens(line)
        Case Ok(toks)
            RckLoadRan(orig, st, line, RckWalk(RckRemember(st), toks, { }), more, out)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckStop(orig, w, Concat(out, RckLoadFailRow(st, line, { }, w)))
        Case Else
            RckStop(orig, "the saved line did not read", Concat(out, RckLoadFailRow(st, line, { }, "the saved line did not read")))

Def RckLoadRan(orig As RckState, before As RckState, line As String, r As RckRun, more As List Of String, out As List Of String) As RckLine
    Select Case r
        Case RckWent(st2, said)
            RckLoadOn(orig, st2, "", more, Concat(out, RckLoadOkRow(st2, line, said)))
        Case RckBroke(w, said)
            RckStop(orig, w, Concat(out, RckLoadFailRow(before, line, said, w)))
        Case Else
            RckStop(orig, "the saved line did not answer", out)

Rem The transcript a LOAD leaves behind — one row shaped exactly like a
Rem typed line's tape row (mark, whatever it said, the stack it left) —
Rem so a caller that wants to show what a load did can, without a second
Rem evaluation. Whether it is shown is the shell's call; RckLoad always
Rem builds it, and still touches no tape.
Def RckLoadOkRow(st2 As RckState, line As String, said As List Of String) As List Of String
    Concat(Prepend(RckTapeMark() & line, said), RckShow(st2))

Def RckLoadFailRow(st As RckState, line As String, said As List Of String, why As String) As List Of String
    Concat(Append(Prepend(RckTapeMark() & line, said), RckWhyMark() & why), RckShow(st))
